WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.110
- Welcome now, officially, I'm Paul North.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.730
I teach critical theory at Yale.

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:17.730
I'm happy to present today's
speaker in the series

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.140
that I'm hosting together

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.100
with my colleague at Ohio
State University, Paul Reitter.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.020
The series is called "The
Value of Marx's Capital".

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:28.380
And these are the Franke lectures,

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.380
at the humanities, at the
Whitney Humanity Center at Yale.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.650
I need to thank the Franke family

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.090
who make possible the Franke lectures.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:40.190
That's Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.430
and these lectures are intended

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.440
to present important
topics in the humanities

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.620
to a wide and general audience.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.750
I need to thank as I
always do Alice Kaplan

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.450
for making this possible

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:53.450
who stepped in to direct
the Whitney Humanity Center,

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:55.400
right when the pandemic hit.

00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.220
And so there's a lot of plans,

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.830
and lots of interesting
things are gonna happen

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:03.370
as time moves on.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.310
I need to thank Sandra Malan-Bowles

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.000
and Leana Hirschfeld Kroen

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.990
for doing the organizational side,

00:01:08.990 --> 00:01:12.400
and Audrey Leak who's
the technical genius,

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.210
and a brilliant undergraduate at Yale.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:17.403
Thank you very much, Audrey,
for running the show.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:22.010
Okay, this is the way it's gonna
work as it has in the past.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.040
We'll have the presentation
from our speaker,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.130
and then we'll take questions.

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.160
I want to give room

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.820
for the students in the seminar

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:34.620
that I'm running on Marx's
"Capital, Volume One"

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.600
time to ask questions, but
I think we'll ask everyone

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.670
to put their questions in the chat

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.850
after the presentation is over.

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.410
So we don't lose track
of them, hang on to them,

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:45.810
write them down,

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:50.810
and I will open up the
floor when things end.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:52.250
I'm really excited

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:55.930
to welcome our guest in
this way of welcoming

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:59.440
where we're just having
faces hanging on screens.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.110
But I have been reading
a quote from a text,

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:05.620
from our speakers as they come in.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:06.453
So I'm gonna start

00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:11.250
with a quote from our
speaker from an early book,

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.970
and an important book
among many important books.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:15.510
Here's the quote.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.327
This is my translation,

00:02:17.327 --> 00:02:21.540
"Marx does not intend a moral
criticism of capitalism.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.390
It is not a question of justice

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:25.160
or the violation of certain norms,

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.070
but rather of the confirmation
of a state of affairs.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.210
That capitalist production damages

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.683
elementary vital interests of workers.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.537
In order to establish this, no," quote,

00:02:37.537 --> 00:02:40.167
"right to an undamaged life," unquote,

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:43.730
"needs to be postulated
or anything like it.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:47.410
Marx wants to show
scientifically, wissenschaftlich,

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.540
that this violation of vital interests

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.420
is inextricably bound up
with the capitalist system.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.960
And in so far as insight
into this characteristic

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.400
of the capitalist system grows

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.870
for the working class it will
also lead to political action.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:02.960
Not in the name of universal justice,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.930
but in its own interest,

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:08.230
at least that is the
optimistic hope of Marx."

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.356
This is a quote from a book

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.610
that was originally published in 1991

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.100
by Michael Heinrich, our guest today

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.470
the book is "Die Wissenschaft vom Wert"

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.090
which is going into a, one

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:24.860
of the multiple additions that
his books tend to go through.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.190
Michael Heinrich is a former collaborator

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:29.630
of the Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.880
and was until 2016 professor of economics

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.573
at the HTW in Berlin.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:36.343
His accomplishments

00:03:36.343 --> 00:03:39.350
on paper are many and his books are by now

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:41.970
some of them in double digits of editions

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:44.840
I hate to look at a long career writing

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.270
in German only from the
English language perspective

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:48.790
but to make this short

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:52.210
for those listening, you can
read several titles in English.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:53.267
First of all

00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:56.640
"An Introduction to the Three
Volumes of Marx's Capital"

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.680
the most reasonable, the most introductory

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:05.750
and undistorted view, I
would say of the volumes

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:08.213
as they need to go together.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:11.340
We can read in English the first volume

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.750
of what I think is a projected

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:14.700
three volume biography of Marx.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.040
Is that right?

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:16.960
- [Michael] Four volumes.

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:18.323
- Four volumes, sorry.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:21.855
And that is published in
English, the first volume

00:04:21.855 --> 00:04:25.160
"Karl Marx and the Birth
of the Modern Society"

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.960
from 2019, a further commentary,

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.531
how to read Marx's "Capital"

00:04:29.531 --> 00:04:32.150
which is an extensive
commentary on the beginning

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:33.900
chapters is in the works

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.493
in English will come out
from Monthly Review Press.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.530
And again, for those of
you who can read German,

00:04:39.530 --> 00:04:40.363
I wanna point you

00:04:40.363 --> 00:04:45.363
to this important leap in
the ballet of the debate

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:48.628
over the theory of value,
"Die Wissenschaft vom Wert"

00:04:48.628 --> 00:04:53.380
from 1991, a really valuable
and important contribution.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:55.030
Thank you for coming Michael.

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:57.683
Welcome, and I'll give
the floor over to you.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:03.230
- Yes, thank you very much
for invitation to speak here.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:08.147
And also thank you very
much for the introduction.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:12.000
I just have to

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.210
add one point to the way you introduced me

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:18.903
the book you mentioned
"Wissenschaft vom Wert"

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:24.760
will also appear in
English finally next year.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:28.830
And it is my thickest book.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:33.830
The first edition from 91 was
considerably extended in 99.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:39.543
And of course the extended
version will appear in English.

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:45.520
So now, also I have to
excuse my poor English.

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:54.750
When I hear myself later I
see how many mistakes I make

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:58.720
but I hope at least the
sense of the sentences

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:00.720
will become clear.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.293
Also the English is a
little bit poor sometimes.

00:06:06.429 --> 00:06:09.150
I was invited here to talk

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:14.150
about Marx value theory
and the original title

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:20.550
of my talk was "Marx
Monetary Value Theory".

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:26.610
I changed a little bit the
title, perhaps you saw it

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:29.900
when I sent the table of contents,

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:34.900
I added reading, "Reading
Marx Monetary Value Theory".

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:40.210
I did this because at first
we really have to talk

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:44.780
about, in which way do we talk

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:49.250
about Marx value theory, very usual

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:51.980
and widespread is a way

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:56.980
of talking that maybe I
present my interpretation.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:02.390
It is opposed to some
alternative interpretations.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:06.750
I support my interpretation
with some quotes from Marx.

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:10.200
My opponent supports his

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:13.380
or her interpretation
with some other quotes.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:18.130
And when I found five quotes
in five of my interpretation

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:20.730
and the opponent found only three quotes

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.830
then it looks like that I am the winner

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:26.150
of the game, like in a soccer game.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:27.900
But this is of course nonsense

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:30.710
to treat the things like this.

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:35.450
Of course, we have to
read cautiously, the texts

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:39.510
but we cannot reduce reading

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:44.030
of finding appropriate quotations.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.480
And in so far, I will start

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.480
with the prerequisites of
discussing Marx value theory.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.940
I think there are two prerequisites.

00:07:58.940 --> 00:08:03.940
The first is a kind of
double historiography.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:06.030
And the other is

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:09.250
that we really need a
critical reading instead

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:13.220
of looking for a last version,
what these points mean,

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.940
I will explain first

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:20.600
and then I will come
to the critical issues

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:24.650
of Marx value theory and the consequences

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:29.650
of these issues for the
understanding of capitalism.

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:34.170
So what I do, I mean this
double historiography

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:39.410
by the way, I used historiography

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:42.150
as translation for the German,
(speaks in foreign language).

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:49.060
I hope it fits more or less.

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:54.590
I think the must put in
a historiography as well

00:08:55.430 --> 00:09:00.430
our own point of view
as well Marx situation

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.373
and his writing about his issues.

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:07.850
So what means historiography

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:12.023
of our own talking
about Marx value theory?

00:09:13.310 --> 00:09:16.100
When we talk about Marx

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:21.100
our views are not only
influenced by our own positions.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.950
They are also influenced
by our historical situation

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:30.760
in a very wide understanding, for example

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:34.710
talking about Marx before
the Russian revolution

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.650
and after was a big difference.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.060
Before the collapse

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:42.760
of the Soviet union and
after is a big difference.

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:46.830
So historical events play a role

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:51.023
but also theoretical events play a role.

00:09:52.204 --> 00:09:55.650
Already in the social democracy

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:59.350
in the very late 19th century

00:09:59.350 --> 00:10:03.350
started a talk about
dialectical materialism

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:07.820
as philosophical basis,
historical materialism

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:12.273
as the approach to history of Marxism.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.510
But we have to have in mind

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:20.510
that all these terms didn't
appear in Marx writings

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:24.010
he never used the term
dialectical materialism.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:29.010
He never used the term
historical materialism

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:30.970
and about Marxism,

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:35.410
he did this famous phrase,
"I am not a Marxist".

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:39.950
And it was not a phrase only it expressed

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:43.320
a distrust against ism.

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:48.320
So Marx not at all wanted
to be the founder of an ism

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:52.610
but nevertheless, such
categories are taken mostly

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:56.270
for granted when we talk about Marx.

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:59.430
And I cannot go deeper in this discussion.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.160
I just want to say behave very

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:05.983
cautious in using such categories.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:10.250
When we now come to discussing value

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:14.970
theory and additional historical

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:21.380
difficulty emerges just a few years

00:11:22.410 --> 00:11:26.600
after Marx published "Capital volume One"

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:29.960
in the year 1867,

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:34.960
the so-called marginal
revolution started in economics.

00:11:36.290 --> 00:11:37.650
Carl Menger in Austria,

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:39.670
Leon Walras in Switzerland

00:11:39.670 --> 00:11:43.150
and William Stanley Jevons in England

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:47.000
published independently studies

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:52.000
promoting the so-called
marginal utility theory.

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:59.010
It was a new approach to bring

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:03.170
utility into economic thinking.

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:06.050
Adam Smiths denied that utility

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:10.220
can explain value the marginalists

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:15.220
with these marginal utility
renewed the approach.

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:20.640
And it lasted only 20 years

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:25.380
that this new approach became dominant

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:30.380
and this had enormous
consequences for the perception

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:34.493
and also for the discussion
of Marx value theory.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:38.340
The tradition of the marginalists

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:45.260
they created a new view on
the possible value theories.

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:47.500
On the one hand, it was said

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.880
we have the so-called objective theories

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:55.570
of value resting on
labor, production costs.

00:12:55.570 --> 00:13:00.463
On the other hand, we have the
subjective theories of value.

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:06.330
They are built on consumer
preferences and utility.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:10.190
In this distinction, Marx

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:12.640
and the classical political economists

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.520
like Adam Smith and David Ricardo are

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:20.520
in one camp the differences
between them are minimized

00:13:22.369 --> 00:13:25.973
for many readers, no matter

00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:32.400
if Marxists or critics of
Marx, the original claim

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:36.940
of Marx to deliver a fundamental critique

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:41.720
of a classical political
economy disappeared.

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:46.120
So it was only the question labor theory

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:51.120
when we talk about value, do
we have a labor theory of value

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:54.860
or do we have a utility theory of value?

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:57.640
But I think this way

00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:02.410
of framing value theory
is totally misleading.

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:06.340
Not only at Marx, already with Smiths.

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:09.620
Smiths justified labor

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:14.620
as a matter of value by
the subjective evaluation

00:14:15.020 --> 00:14:19.730
of the producer, where he said,
"Yes, labor is the real cost

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:23.520
because it provokes misery to the producer

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:28.027
and he will not have an
exchange of a labor product

00:14:28.027 --> 00:14:32.060
with something else which
he can get for less labor".

00:14:32.060 --> 00:14:37.060
So Adam Smiths in a subjective way

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:39.170
gave reason to the so-called

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:43.870
objective approach to value theory.

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:48.870
So I think all these
distinctions, objective subjective

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:55.030
labor theories, utility
theories have only a very,

00:14:55.030 --> 00:14:58.630
very limited use, but nevertheless

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:03.630
they are used in a way as if
we can take them for granted.

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:13.670
Last point I want to
mention what is important

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:16.530
for our present perception

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:20.470
of Marx or of, not of
Marx theory in general,

00:15:20.470 --> 00:15:22.407
but of "Capital".

00:15:22.407 --> 00:15:26.970
"Capital" as we know, three volumes.

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:31.970
Unfortunately, most people
read only volume one,

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:34.600
but the three volumes of capital

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:38.910
form a unit, very important categories

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:43.910
like profit and interest
appear only in volume three.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:48.080
When you don't know what
Marx means with profit

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:51.970
you will automatically tend to confuse

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:56.970
surplus value, category
of volume one, with profit

00:15:57.110 --> 00:16:02.110
and entrepreneurial gain,
categories of volume three.

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:08.750
So we really need all
three volumes to read.

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:12.410
But when we look to history volume one

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:15.713
appeared in the year 1867.

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:21.470
Volume three after Marx
death edited by his friend

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:26.310
Frederick Engels appeared
nearly 30 years later in 1894.

00:16:28.640 --> 00:16:33.640
And while volume one was nicely finished

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:37.780
by Marx was nicely presented by Marx,

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:42.780
volume two and three
Engels had to construct out

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.410
of unfinished manuscripts and to

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:50.410
read it is much more difficult
than to read volume one.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:55.790
As a result, since 100
years, we have the situation

00:16:55.790 --> 00:17:00.790
that volume one dominates
the reception of "Capital".

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:03.440
Volume one deals with

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:08.367
the production process of
capital and many Marxists

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:14.690
as well as Marx critics belief
here we find the real core

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:19.750
of Marx analysis of
capitalism and what is said

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:24.500
in volume two or three are some
details, additional details.

00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:29.500
We must not go too much in
depth occupying with them.

00:17:30.160 --> 00:17:33.770
And I think this is a
very wrong interpretation

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:38.770
a very wrong accentuation of capital.

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:44.720
So these were some very brief remarks

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:49.030
about the historiography
of our own position

00:17:49.030 --> 00:17:54.030
that we have to be cautious
to take things for granted

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:58.439
which are the result of a
certain historical process.

00:17:58.439 --> 00:18:02.752
We just shouldn't trust what is offered

00:18:02.752 --> 00:18:06.923
as a categories, things
we can take for granted.

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:08.953
Now I come

00:18:08.953 --> 00:18:13.330
to my second point, the historiography

00:18:13.330 --> 00:18:16.433
of Marx analysis of value.

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:24.340
In general, we have to put Marx

00:18:24.410 --> 00:18:29.410
theoretical texts, much more
in the conflict of his times

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:33.730
than this is usually done.

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:36.010
Most readers of "Capital",

00:18:36.010 --> 00:18:40.940
they want to learn something
about contemporary capitalism.

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:41.773
It is said,

00:18:41.773 --> 00:18:44.030
"Yes, Marx is an important author.

00:18:44.030 --> 00:18:48.210
He has something to say,
read it," and they read it.

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:53.150
But most readers are
not clear about the fact

00:18:53.150 --> 00:18:58.150
that all Marx texts also
"Capital" is an intervention

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:04.320
in debates, in conflicts,
in struggles of his times.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:09.320
It is not a timeless
analytical effort to contribute

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:12.750
to the wisdom of human mankind.

00:19:13.630 --> 00:19:16.990
And so we have to reconstruct all

00:19:20.370 --> 00:19:22.770
these contemporary, for Marx,

00:19:22.770 --> 00:19:25.600
contemporary struggles and debates

00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:30.600
in order to get a deeper
understanding of the writings.

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:35.040
And you mentioned my Marx biography.

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:39.580
This is one of the main
tasks of this biography.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:44.580
It is not to tell you
every detail of Marx life

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:49.750
and what was his dinner

00:19:49.750 --> 00:19:52.810
on Sunday evening or something like that.

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:57.280
No, I think we need the biography

00:19:57.280 --> 00:20:02.210
for really have the context

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:05.230
of his writings, to
understand the conflicts

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:09.990
to understand also Marx
standing in these conflicts

00:20:09.990 --> 00:20:14.990
which was changing, Marx
was all his lifetime

00:20:15.230 --> 00:20:20.230
an extremely learning
subject who had no problems

00:20:21.819 --> 00:20:24.970
to admit that something

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:29.970
what he wrote or said
two years ago was wrong.

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:32.870
That he had now a new insight

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:38.080
which has to be taken into account.

00:20:38.080 --> 00:20:42.270
This, by the way, is also
a reason that why so many

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:49.420
of Marx works including
capital were not finished

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:52.210
because he was permanently learning.

00:20:52.210 --> 00:20:55.320
He did not just fulfill a program

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:59.720
and sent the manuscript to the reader.

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:02.090
So just for "Capital"

00:21:02.090 --> 00:21:07.060
I think I can say that it
was not finished in the 70s.

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:09.060
It had of course,

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:11.640
one of the reasons were that Marx was sick

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:15.270
in the 70s, that also in the

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:19.330
in the early 70s he was
politically very engaged,

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:22.950
very active, but also in the 1870s

00:21:24.520 --> 00:21:29.520
he extended the plan for
"Capital" considerably.

00:21:31.330 --> 00:21:36.330
And I would say he could
extended it even so much

00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:39.730
that even when he lived 10 years more

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:42.210
and would have the best health,

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:45.230
he wouldn't be able to finish

00:21:45.230 --> 00:21:49.730
this extremely ambitious project.

00:21:49.730 --> 00:21:52.850
And also this, we have to
have in mind when we discuss

00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:57.850
about "Capital" that we have
not one finished project

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:01.000
we have an evolving project.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:05.990
We have to see how things are developing.

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:10.560
I will come back to this point in short.

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:15.560
Now I just want to give you
a very, very rough idea.

00:22:15.584 --> 00:22:19.137
What were the struggles behind "Capital".

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:25.100
Marx of course dealt with the
existing political economy,

00:22:25.630 --> 00:22:27.650
this political economy was one

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:32.650
of the main justifying
sciences for capitalism.

00:22:33.830 --> 00:22:38.670
So it was not just a scientific debate.

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:42.240
It was a debate about the science

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:47.240
which has central
importance for capitalism.

00:22:48.230 --> 00:22:49.590
And in this times

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:54.140
he, in "Capital", he made
the famous distinction

00:22:54.140 --> 00:22:58.250
between classical political
economy on the one hand

00:22:58.250 --> 00:23:02.350
which he accepted as a scientific approach

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:05.680
a bourgeois approach but
nevertheless a scientific approach

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:10.680
and vulgar economics, which
was not really scientific.

00:23:11.310 --> 00:23:14.140
It remained on the surface

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:17.373
and in the course of
time, more and more this,

00:23:18.383 --> 00:23:22.330
what he called vulgar
economics became more

00:23:22.330 --> 00:23:25.330
and more apologetic.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:27.130
It was clear for the writers

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:29.640
we have to defend capitalism

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:32.810
and we have to present capitalism in a way

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:35.883
that it is the best world ever.

00:23:36.977 --> 00:23:39.820
But there is a third branch

00:23:39.820 --> 00:23:42.780
not so Marx made it not so prominent

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:46.500
but you can can see it in some footnotes

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:47.803
in some remarks,

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:53.980
this was especially the
German economists of his time.

00:23:55.890 --> 00:23:58.330
He considered German economists

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:02.510
of his time, even worse
than the vulgar economists.

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:06.810
Germany developed rather
late, economically.

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:11.810
It was behind England and
France, in the first half

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:13.990
of the 19th century.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:18.100
So there was not really an empirical basis

00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:20.800
for doing political economy.

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:25.780
And the German economists took some,

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:29.190
some fragments from Adam Smith,

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:34.190
Ricardo say which they
not even really understood

00:24:36.883 --> 00:24:37.716
They mixed it up

00:24:37.716 --> 00:24:42.670
with some German historical consideration.

00:24:44.210 --> 00:24:49.210
And the result for Marx was
not at all a serious project.

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:56.290
Another frontline for Marx

00:24:56.290 --> 00:25:01.290
were competing socialist
thinkers, for example

00:25:05.510 --> 00:25:10.470
Proudhon who he mentioned
several times in "Capital".

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:15.470
They also made use of
classical political economy

00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:19.010
in a way which Marx criticized very much.

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:22.430
So Marx had to fight

00:25:22.430 --> 00:25:27.430
against very different
camps in his "Capital".

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:32.940
I will mention later what this means

00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:34.663
for certain points.

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:41.240
But now I have to
mention in this paragraph

00:25:41.550 --> 00:25:46.550
just one point regarding the
publication of "Capital".

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:51.780
I said the German

00:25:51.780 --> 00:25:56.490
economists were even worse
then the vulgar economists

00:25:56.490 --> 00:26:01.490
in England and France,
but in which language

00:26:02.130 --> 00:26:05.570
and where "Capital" was
published in Germany

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.977
Marx in his letters said,

00:26:09.977 --> 00:26:13.500
"Oh, nobody really discusses 'Capital'".

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:17.163
And he talked about a
conspiracy of silence.

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:21.380
My idea is there was
not so much a conspiracy

00:26:21.380 --> 00:26:23.600
of silence against the book.

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:27.630
There was the German
economists in the majority.

00:26:27.630 --> 00:26:30.730
They're really unable
to understand the book

00:26:30.730 --> 00:26:34.640
they were before unable
to understand really

00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:36.600
classical political economy.

00:26:36.600 --> 00:26:38.690
And so they didn't understand the critique

00:26:38.690 --> 00:26:40.513
of classical political economy.

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:45.640
They also thought that it is
not necessary to criticize

00:26:45.640 --> 00:26:49.530
because the critic of the Germans

00:26:49.530 --> 00:26:53.660
was, "Oh, David Ricardo is too abstract.

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:56.440
We cannot use such an abstract theory."

00:26:56.440 --> 00:26:59.227
And this was reason enough to say,

00:26:59.227 --> 00:27:01.507
"Oh, we must not occupy it".

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:06.500
but now you can say,
okay, Marx book was not

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:10.750
it didn't aim to the
academic debate in Germany.

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:14.410
It was a book with socialist aims.

00:27:14.410 --> 00:27:19.370
It was written to support
the proletarian relation

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:21.363
the proletarian revolution.

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:24.263
A book for workers.

00:27:25.880 --> 00:27:29.600
But could the workers
really make use of "Capital"

00:27:29.600 --> 00:27:34.600
in this time, they were first
the selling price of the book.

00:27:36.010 --> 00:27:39.830
The first edition had a price which was

00:27:39.830 --> 00:27:44.830
around the weekly income
of a skilled worker.

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:49.620
So far the for the most
workers in Germany,

00:27:49.620 --> 00:27:52.083
the book was not affordable.

00:27:53.060 --> 00:27:56.820
In the first edition, also Marx didn't.

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:59.510
And also in, in later editions

00:27:59.510 --> 00:28:04.510
he didn't translate the
quotes from foreign languages.

00:28:04.510 --> 00:28:07.960
You could find five different languages

00:28:07.960 --> 00:28:10.630
in "Capital", ancient Greek, Latin,

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:12.513
English, French, and Italian.

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:18.620
He used a lot of terms
from ancient mythology,

00:28:19.420 --> 00:28:23.070
philosophy, world literature, and so on.

00:28:23.070 --> 00:28:25.727
So it was really a book

00:28:25.727 --> 00:28:29.900
for that educated, very
well educated reader.

00:28:29.900 --> 00:28:34.113
And these were in these
times, mainly the bourgeoisie.

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:38.530
So it is not surprising

00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:41.210
that his book was not really a success

00:28:41.210 --> 00:28:44.070
in the first years after it was published.

00:28:44.070 --> 00:28:46.100
And when finally, 20 years

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:51.100
after the first edition, an
English translation appeared

00:28:53.930 --> 00:28:56.270
then Marx opponent classical

00:28:56.270 --> 00:29:00.820
political economy already had vanished.

00:29:00.820 --> 00:29:02.950
Marginalists were ruling.

00:29:02.950 --> 00:29:04.436
So the book looked

00:29:04.436 --> 00:29:09.436
like a relic of an old
time, which has no meaning

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:16.663
for the present times.

00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:22.590
So regarded under the
marketing aspects "Capital"

00:29:22.590 --> 00:29:27.093
during the first 20 years
was really a disaster.

00:29:29.480 --> 00:29:34.163
And also of course, this then
influenced the reception.

00:29:35.890 --> 00:29:37.770
Now I want to come

00:29:37.770 --> 00:29:42.770
to my next point before I then really come

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:47.720
to the critical issues of value theory.

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:50.220
I imagined that we mean

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:55.220
that we need a critical
reading of Marx texts.

00:29:56.370 --> 00:29:59.640
There is not only in
reading Marx, but also

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:04.410
in reading a lot of other
so-called classical authors

00:30:04.410 --> 00:30:09.410
a tendency to look for a last
version where the author said

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:14.490
what he really want to say.

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:19.040
In most cases, the search
for such an illusionary,

00:30:19.040 --> 00:30:23.093
for such a last version
is really illusionary.

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:28.210
In Germany, this insight is rather old

00:30:28.210 --> 00:30:33.210
and it led to the
so-called critical editions

00:30:33.890 --> 00:30:38.800
not only to edit the last,
the so-called last version

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:43.800
of a book, but to publish
all the versions of the book

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:50.300
to publish also all the
changes in a single text.

00:30:53.620 --> 00:30:58.003
In Germany, we have fortunately

00:30:58.003 --> 00:31:02.436
the Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe

00:31:02.436 --> 00:31:05.680
which it is not finished

00:31:05.680 --> 00:31:10.680
but which gives such a critical
edition, which then need

00:31:11.860 --> 00:31:16.383
which then makes possible
such a critical reading.

00:31:18.160 --> 00:31:23.160
What this critical reading had, the impact

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:25.260
of this critical reading

00:31:25.260 --> 00:31:30.020
even for volume one, I

00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:36.830
to illustrate this, I
just give you one example.

00:31:37.010 --> 00:31:39.950
You can hear very often the opinion

00:31:39.950 --> 00:31:42.050
that the French translation

00:31:42.050 --> 00:31:46.920
of volume one is the last version

00:31:46.920 --> 00:31:49.500
because chronologically it appeared

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:52.210
after the first two German editions.

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:54.770
And it was really the last edition

00:31:57.930 --> 00:31:59.850
on which Marx was working.

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:02.280
He checked the translation

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:06.437
and changed a lot of points
and even Marx wrote, yes,

00:32:06.437 --> 00:32:09.357
"When you can read German, nevertheless,"

00:32:10.270 --> 00:32:13.360
to the French readers he
said, "Even when you can read

00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:17.370
German, you should read
this French edition.

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:22.370
It has a scientific value of its own".

00:32:24.740 --> 00:32:28.410
But this is not the full truths.

00:32:28.410 --> 00:32:33.410
The French edition brings
a lot of new insights,

00:32:34.830 --> 00:32:38.600
new material, for example, in
the section on accumulation

00:32:39.570 --> 00:32:43.113
but in the section on
value, it's a disaster.

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:48.420
And this also was confirmed by Marx

00:32:48.420 --> 00:32:51.410
when Danielson the translator

00:32:51.410 --> 00:32:55.640
for the Russian edition
asked what is best to do.

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:58.430
Marx told him use the second

00:32:58.430 --> 00:33:02.590
German edition compare with
the French edition, but not

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:04.370
for the first chapters,

00:33:04.370 --> 00:33:08.253
here you have to take
only the German edition.

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:11.380
What happened with these first chapters

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:15.700
in the French translation was simply

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:19.480
that Marx was not at all satisfied

00:33:19.480 --> 00:33:22.590
with the translation of
the French translator

00:33:22.590 --> 00:33:26.890
but he had not the time to
find better translations.

00:33:26.890 --> 00:33:31.890
So in many cases, he
just erased sentences,

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:36.320
half sentences, half paragraphs.

00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:41.320
When my commentary to the first
few chapters was translated

00:33:41.360 --> 00:33:43.970
in France, there was a big problem

00:33:43.970 --> 00:33:48.070
that in this standard translation

00:33:48.070 --> 00:33:52.010
a lot of sentences I
commented they didn't exist

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:55.073
in this book.

00:33:56.037 --> 00:33:58.780
In newer translations

00:33:58.780 --> 00:34:02.530
they added the sentences
as an appendix, but

00:34:02.530 --> 00:34:06.560
as you can imagine, most readers
didn't read the appendix.

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:08.860
So the translation

00:34:08.860 --> 00:34:13.860
or this so-called last
version was really a problem

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:16.957
for understanding "Capital".

00:34:18.030 --> 00:34:23.030
So now this was a long introduction

00:34:24.230 --> 00:34:28.070
but I think it a necessary introduction.

00:34:28.070 --> 00:34:33.070
I come to some critical
points of Marx value theory.

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:41.000
Many Marxists, as many Marx
critics have a very simple view

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:43.350
on value theory.

00:34:43.350 --> 00:34:47.560
When you ask them, what's
tells you value theory

00:34:47.560 --> 00:34:51.623
they say, "Okay, labor creates value.

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:57.180
The magnitude of labor spent

00:34:57.180 --> 00:35:01.600
in production gives us
the magnitude of value."

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:02.510
So it looks

00:35:02.510 --> 00:35:07.510
like that production
already determines value

00:35:07.800 --> 00:35:12.040
and its magnitude and
money is just an expression

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:17.040
of value which exists also
without regarding money.

00:35:19.770 --> 00:35:24.770
All these propositions I just quoted

00:35:25.830 --> 00:35:30.830
in a briefly way are
very, very problematic.

00:35:33.850 --> 00:35:38.790
And this, I want to show
now in this third part

00:35:38.790 --> 00:35:42.363
about the critical points
of Marx value theory.

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:48.300
First, we have to be clear about Marx

00:35:48.300 --> 00:35:52.550
way of presenting things.

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:55.190
For example, when you read
the first two chapters

00:35:55.190 --> 00:35:58.460
of "Capital" many readers are puzzled

00:35:59.465 --> 00:36:03.190
in chapter one in value form analysis

00:36:03.190 --> 00:36:05.180
you find money in chapter two

00:36:05.180 --> 00:36:06.890
you again can find money.

00:36:06.890 --> 00:36:08.930
What's the difference?

00:36:08.930 --> 00:36:13.787
And many say, okay, even you
can read this in commentaries,

00:36:13.787 --> 00:36:16.950
"The presentation in chapter two

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:19.977
is a more concrete presentation."

00:36:20.900 --> 00:36:21.940
This is nonsense.

00:36:21.940 --> 00:36:26.590
There is a very clear
division between chapter one

00:36:26.590 --> 00:36:31.590
and chapter two, chapter one
presents the form determination

00:36:33.800 --> 00:36:38.170
and chapter two presents human action

00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:40.900
ruled by this form determination

00:36:42.600 --> 00:36:46.170
probably most of you
remember this famous sentence

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:49.890
from the preface that persons that

00:36:49.890 --> 00:36:53.110
Marx criticizes the actions of capitalists

00:36:53.110 --> 00:36:58.110
and land owners, but he is
not dealing with the character

00:36:58.830 --> 00:37:03.680
of these persons because
they don't play a role

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:08.680
as persons but as personifications
of economic categories.

00:37:13.190 --> 00:37:16.700
Probably, you know, the sentence
you all know the sentence.

00:37:16.700 --> 00:37:18.240
Maybe you quoted it.

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:23.087
You used it, but this sentence implies

00:37:23.960 --> 00:37:27.300
a very ambitious program.

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:31.810
When I really want to present persons

00:37:31.810 --> 00:37:36.810
as personifications of economic categories

00:37:37.650 --> 00:37:42.650
I must present first, the
economic categories independently

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:44.510
from the persons.

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:49.510
This already is a scientific
program in total contrast

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:54.584
to the program of
classical political economy

00:37:54.584 --> 00:37:59.584
as well, neo-classical political
economy, because both start

00:38:00.140 --> 00:38:05.140
with the individual and
the needs, the desire

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:12.110
or the possibilities of the individual,

00:38:12.110 --> 00:38:15.050
and only from the action of the individual

00:38:15.050 --> 00:38:17.803
they derive economic institutions.

00:38:19.050 --> 00:38:24.050
Marx goes the opposite way in chapter one.

00:38:25.790 --> 00:38:30.790
His object is the exchange
relation of commodities.

00:38:33.300 --> 00:38:38.167
In chapter two, his object
is the exchange process

00:38:39.360 --> 00:38:43.990
of commodity owners, and they do this

00:38:43.990 --> 00:38:48.990
following their free
will, but this free will

00:38:49.280 --> 00:38:53.810
when they want to avoid
economic bankruptcy has to

00:38:53.810 --> 00:38:58.810
follow the form determinations
analyzed in chapter one.

00:39:00.610 --> 00:39:05.610
So, and this pattern also exists later

00:39:06.560 --> 00:39:11.560
in chapter four Marx analyzes
"Capital" and only this

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:15.230
in a very short remark

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:19.760
he comes to the capitalist
who makes the objective aim

00:39:19.760 --> 00:39:23.693
of capital to his or her subjective aim.

00:39:25.130 --> 00:39:29.640
So this we have to have
in mind, do we speak

00:39:29.640 --> 00:39:33.140
about form determinations
or do we speak about action?

00:39:33.140 --> 00:39:36.130
It makes a big difference for Marx.

00:39:36.130 --> 00:39:37.060
- Michael.

00:39:37.060 --> 00:39:39.010
Can I ask you a question?
- Of course.

00:39:40.160 --> 00:39:41.530
- At this point

00:39:41.530 --> 00:39:44.840
I would like to know your opinion

00:39:44.840 --> 00:39:49.340
on this choice of Marx
to do science in this way

00:39:49.340 --> 00:39:51.160
to start with the categories

00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:53.550
to imagine that persons
are personifications

00:39:53.550 --> 00:39:57.250
of categories, where does that come from?

00:39:57.250 --> 00:39:58.700
Or let me ask you this.

00:39:58.700 --> 00:40:01.550
What in "Capital" makes it right

00:40:01.550 --> 00:40:04.303
that you make this scientific choice?

00:40:06.180 --> 00:40:07.420
- Okay.

00:40:07.420 --> 00:40:10.330
These are two different
question where it comes from

00:40:10.330 --> 00:40:12.650
is more or less biographically

00:40:12.650 --> 00:40:17.630
a question where which,
where the learning processes

00:40:17.630 --> 00:40:21.980
of Marx to do this because in the 1840s

00:40:23.410 --> 00:40:28.220
it was not so clear this difference.

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:33.220
I think it started in early 1850s

00:40:33.300 --> 00:40:37.420
in his notebooks where he
was working day after day

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:40.593
in the British museum in London.

00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:44.480
To justify this program

00:40:45.340 --> 00:40:50.340
we just have to see if the analysis works.

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:56.610
When we look, for example, in chapter two

00:40:56.610 --> 00:41:01.590
in "Capital" already Marx tries to explain

00:41:01.590 --> 00:41:04.750
on a rather general abstract level

00:41:04.750 --> 00:41:08.090
what commodity owners have to do.

00:41:08.090 --> 00:41:13.090
And there we can check is
this analysis convincing

00:41:13.720 --> 00:41:17.980
that what they have to do is exactly

00:41:17.980 --> 00:41:22.980
the form determination
analyzed in chapter one.

00:41:23.770 --> 00:41:25.760
We have to discuss this.

00:41:25.760 --> 00:41:28.327
And here I, this is just a

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:36.950
in brackets set, the first
edition, the value form analysis

00:41:36.950 --> 00:41:41.950
of the first edition in this
respect is much more precise

00:41:42.420 --> 00:41:45.304
than in the second edition

00:41:45.304 --> 00:41:50.304
the first edition starts
with a different force

00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:54.210
value form not already
with the money form, but

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:59.210
with a kind of crazy value form where

00:41:59.510 --> 00:42:04.320
every commodity is the general equivalent.

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:06.630
But exactly this appears

00:42:06.630 --> 00:42:11.630
in chapter two, where Marx,
I think very clearly states

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:16.650
what do the commodity owners desire?

00:42:16.650 --> 00:42:20.000
Every commodity owner has the desire

00:42:20.000 --> 00:42:24.560
that his or her commodity
is the general equivalent.

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:27.780
So the crazy force value form

00:42:27.780 --> 00:42:31.710
of the first edition is exactly the desire

00:42:31.710 --> 00:42:36.710
of the commodity owners,
which is not realistic.

00:42:38.330 --> 00:42:42.210
And therefore they have to come to money.

00:42:42.210 --> 00:42:44.600
So the program

00:42:44.600 --> 00:42:48.950
I think is convincing of
Marx, but this doesn't mean

00:42:48.950 --> 00:42:53.950
that the way Marx presented
this program is correct

00:42:56.810 --> 00:43:01.810
or convincing in every detail,
there is some work to do.

00:43:04.130 --> 00:43:06.610
So now I want to come

00:43:06.610 --> 00:43:11.610
to the next critical point,
double character of labor.

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:15.980
Everybody knows this really

00:43:17.590 --> 00:43:22.390
in an audience I would
ask, "Where is my mistake

00:43:22.390 --> 00:43:25.560
when I say a double character of labor?"

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:28.600
Marx didn't speak of
double character of labor.

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:31.450
His book about the double character

00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:36.240
of labor represented in commodities.

00:43:36.240 --> 00:43:41.240
Double character is not
a not historical feature

00:43:41.880 --> 00:43:43.293
of any labor.

00:43:44.390 --> 00:43:49.390
It is a feature of labor
represented in commodities.

00:43:49.970 --> 00:43:54.150
So it is a historical
feature only existing

00:43:56.960 --> 00:44:00.770
in a commodity producing society.

00:44:00.770 --> 00:44:04.270
Here again, we have a decisive critique

00:44:04.270 --> 00:44:07.970
against Adam Smith and
the classical school

00:44:07.970 --> 00:44:11.847
because they not only don't do this

00:44:13.270 --> 00:44:15.520
differentiation double character

00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:20.520
abstract and concrete labor,
they confuse concrete labor,

00:44:21.860 --> 00:44:24.660
what we have in every society

00:44:24.660 --> 00:44:28.680
with abstract labor, what
belongs to commodity production.

00:44:28.680 --> 00:44:33.340
And by this confusion, they
make commodity production

00:44:33.340 --> 00:44:36.420
to something which has to exist always.

00:44:36.420 --> 00:44:37.560
And the laws

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:41.130
of commodity production of
capitalist commodity production

00:44:41.130 --> 00:44:45.763
for Adam Smith are the
loss of the economy.

00:44:47.320 --> 00:44:52.320
So, this is a very critical
distinction critic against

00:44:56.720 --> 00:45:01.720
the classical school, but
also it is very important

00:45:02.710 --> 00:45:05.340
for the later use in "Capital"

00:45:05.340 --> 00:45:07.360
when you come to the difference

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:10.660
for example, between
constant and variable capital

00:45:10.660 --> 00:45:14.850
again, plays this double
character, a decisive role.

00:45:14.850 --> 00:45:16.340
But what I want to stress here

00:45:16.340 --> 00:45:21.340
in connecting this value theory is

00:45:21.360 --> 00:45:26.360
that how did we find abstract labor?

00:45:26.840 --> 00:45:30.200
We needed the exchange process,
in the exchange process

00:45:30.200 --> 00:45:35.200
there is an abstraction from use value.

00:45:36.610 --> 00:45:37.810
When we abstract

00:45:37.810 --> 00:45:42.440
from every use value, then
automatically the labor changes.

00:45:42.440 --> 00:45:44.700
It is not any more, a concrete labor.

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:47.080
It is a kind of abstract labor.

00:45:47.080 --> 00:45:50.970
It is not an abstraction done consciously

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:55.610
by someone, it is a practical
abstraction existing

00:45:57.050 --> 00:45:59.400
in everyday practice.

00:45:59.400 --> 00:46:04.400
But this means that abstract
labor is connected to exchange.

00:46:06.130 --> 00:46:08.800
When we only consider production

00:46:08.800 --> 00:46:12.010
we cannot talk about abstract labor

00:46:12.010 --> 00:46:15.440
and not talk about value.

00:46:15.440 --> 00:46:18.900
The same, by the way, is true regarding

00:46:18.900 --> 00:46:20.983
the magnitude of value.

00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:26.050
You all know the famous definition

00:46:26.050 --> 00:46:31.050
from the first five, six
pages of the first chapter

00:46:32.270 --> 00:46:35.990
that not the individually spent

00:46:35.990 --> 00:46:39.640
labor time forms the magnitude

00:46:39.640 --> 00:46:44.640
of value, but only socially
necessary labor time.

00:46:46.120 --> 00:46:49.000
But what is socially necessary labor time?

00:46:49.000 --> 00:46:50.420
It is labor time

00:46:50.420 --> 00:46:55.420
which in average, at a
certain situation is necessary

00:46:57.250 --> 00:47:01.540
but which labor enters this average

00:47:01.540 --> 00:47:05.500
not all the labor spent in production

00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:10.500
but only that labor whose
products enter the market.

00:47:13.370 --> 00:47:16.070
When you have a perfect factory

00:47:16.070 --> 00:47:19.680
which will not deliver
products to the market

00:47:20.610 --> 00:47:22.160
the labor conditions of this

00:47:22.160 --> 00:47:26.740
factory will not influence
socially necessary labor time.

00:47:26.740 --> 00:47:29.010
So also about the magnitude

00:47:29.010 --> 00:47:34.010
of value, we can only speak
regarding not only production

00:47:34.270 --> 00:47:38.283
but production and exchange.

00:47:40.970 --> 00:47:45.310
The third of these critical
points, value form.

00:47:45.310 --> 00:47:49.310
I already mentioned it shortly, value form

00:47:49.310 --> 00:47:54.310
in the first chapter from
simple value form to money form.

00:47:54.430 --> 00:47:57.550
Very often, it is misunderstood

00:47:57.550 --> 00:48:02.550
as a kind of abbreviated
historical development of money.

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:11.280
But when you read cautiously already

00:48:11.280 --> 00:48:16.280
the introductionary part of
this sub chapter on value form

00:48:18.450 --> 00:48:21.640
then you can see Marx is
not speaking about money.

00:48:21.640 --> 00:48:22.473
He is speaking

00:48:22.473 --> 00:48:27.440
about the money form, the
necessity of the money form.

00:48:27.440 --> 00:48:32.440
The necessity of a form
is not a historical event.

00:48:33.060 --> 00:48:34.180
And the necessity

00:48:34.180 --> 00:48:38.680
of a form relates to
the system of categories

00:48:38.680 --> 00:48:43.680
with which I describe
an existing totality.

00:48:43.770 --> 00:48:46.530
Is it necessary to have this form

00:48:46.530 --> 00:48:50.020
in order to describe,
to analyze this totality

00:48:50.020 --> 00:48:50.963
or is it not?

00:48:52.210 --> 00:48:57.210
The background of this
seemingly abstract reasoning

00:48:58.430 --> 00:48:59.723
is very political.

00:49:00.950 --> 00:49:05.870
When Marx started with
"Grundrisse", he was occupied

00:49:05.870 --> 00:49:10.870
with a pupil of Proudhon, a
French author named D'Adamo

00:49:11.830 --> 00:49:16.770
and there he discussed, D'Adamo
discussed Proudhon thesis,

00:49:17.980 --> 00:49:22.980
what is socialism, and Marx
went into this discussion.

00:49:24.430 --> 00:49:27.980
Proudhon's basic idea
was to introduce a kind

00:49:27.980 --> 00:49:32.763
of commodity production without money.

00:49:33.620 --> 00:49:35.940
And this gave reason to Marx

00:49:35.940 --> 00:49:40.000
to think about the connection
between commodity and money.

00:49:40.000 --> 00:49:44.750
Is it only a superficial
connection so that you can get rid

00:49:44.750 --> 00:49:48.010
of money, or is it much deeper?

00:49:48.010 --> 00:49:51.425
His first result, Marx
first result presented

00:49:51.425 --> 00:49:56.425
in 1859 in a contribution to the critique

00:49:56.560 --> 00:50:01.560
of political economy
was a kind of confusion.

00:50:01.910 --> 00:50:05.460
He confounded analysis of value form

00:50:05.460 --> 00:50:08.750
with analysis of the exchange process.

00:50:08.750 --> 00:50:12.140
He himself discovered these shortcomings

00:50:12.140 --> 00:50:17.140
in early sixties when he works
on theories of surplus value

00:50:17.770 --> 00:50:21.290
and he occupied with Samuel Bailey,

00:50:21.290 --> 00:50:26.290
and English economists
in early 19th century.

00:50:27.430 --> 00:50:32.430
Bailey criticized Ricardian
theories of value.

00:50:33.330 --> 00:50:38.330
Bailey said, "Value always is
only a relational category,"

00:50:40.700 --> 00:50:43.640
in the sense of relative value.

00:50:43.640 --> 00:50:48.640
And the Ricardians try to
transform a relational thing

00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:54.693
in an absolute thing,
which is not existing.

00:50:55.950 --> 00:51:00.660
And now Marx wanted on the
one hand to criticize Bailey.

00:51:00.660 --> 00:51:03.300
But on the other hand, he also saw

00:51:03.300 --> 00:51:08.300
that the Ricardians were
unable to criticize Bailey.

00:51:09.130 --> 00:51:14.130
So this showed that there
was a massive mistake,

00:51:14.210 --> 00:51:19.050
massive shortcoming in the
classical Ricardian theory.

00:51:19.050 --> 00:51:20.880
And by this

00:51:20.880 --> 00:51:24.970
he learned that also he
himself did a certain confusion

00:51:24.970 --> 00:51:27.710
in the book of 59.

00:51:27.710 --> 00:51:32.710
And this gave then reason for
the first full presentation

00:51:35.330 --> 00:51:40.330
of value form analyzes in the
first edition of "Capital".

00:51:41.320 --> 00:51:45.080
But because this first presentation

00:51:45.080 --> 00:51:48.600
of value form analysis was so

00:51:48.600 --> 00:51:52.460
difficult to read, Marx was criticized

00:51:52.460 --> 00:51:54.710
by Engels and by his
friend, Louis Cooperman.

00:51:55.710 --> 00:51:58.350
And it was said, nobody will understand.

00:51:58.350 --> 00:52:03.350
Marx then produced a much simpler version

00:52:03.730 --> 00:52:06.180
as an appendix to the first edition.

00:52:06.180 --> 00:52:08.090
And what we now read

00:52:08.090 --> 00:52:12.160
in the standard editions of
"Capital" is much more close

00:52:12.160 --> 00:52:17.160
to the popularized appendix

00:52:17.380 --> 00:52:22.000
than to the strict
presentation of first edition.

00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:24.450
So I just mentioned this

00:52:24.450 --> 00:52:29.020
that you see you have on the
one hand, a certain progress

00:52:29.020 --> 00:52:30.730
in March Marx analysis

00:52:31.630 --> 00:52:34.550
but sometimes you have also a kind

00:52:34.550 --> 00:52:39.550
of regress of introducing
some popularization

00:52:42.010 --> 00:52:45.450
which makes the things less clear.

00:52:45.450 --> 00:52:48.200
And also this is an argument in value form

00:52:48.200 --> 00:52:50.160
analysis very clearly

00:52:50.160 --> 00:52:55.160
there is not one best
version of value analysis,

00:52:55.660 --> 00:52:57.870
of value form analysis.

00:52:57.870 --> 00:52:59.610
When you really want to work

00:52:59.610 --> 00:53:03.373
on it, you need all
the different versions.

00:53:05.460 --> 00:53:09.760
Now, my next point is fateishism.

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:15.620
Also here, we have an
evolution in Marx thinking

00:53:15.620 --> 00:53:18.740
it evolved rather lately.

00:53:18.740 --> 00:53:22.600
I don't give you the

00:53:23.890 --> 00:53:27.660
the history of this because
I always look to the clock

00:53:27.660 --> 00:53:32.660
and the time is running, in first edition

00:53:33.080 --> 00:53:38.080
finally, the, it is
treated more extensively

00:53:38.740 --> 00:53:43.180
but only in the second
edition fateish character

00:53:43.180 --> 00:53:48.180
of the commodity is put
together to one section.

00:53:49.930 --> 00:53:54.930
It is a rather long section,
but we can say nearly

00:53:56.430 --> 00:54:01.430
until the second half of 20th
century, it was mainly ignored

00:54:02.140 --> 00:54:07.140
in discussion from Marxists
as well from Marx critics.

00:54:08.090 --> 00:54:09.830
The interest started mainly

00:54:09.830 --> 00:54:13.210
in the second half of 20th century

00:54:13.210 --> 00:54:18.210
but then very often
fateishism was read as a kind

00:54:18.320 --> 00:54:23.320
of wrong consciousness, there
is a deceiving of people.

00:54:26.470 --> 00:54:31.470
They believe that value
is an objective property

00:54:32.120 --> 00:54:34.023
of things, but it is not.

00:54:36.170 --> 00:54:40.050
But this is really a misunderstanding

00:54:40.050 --> 00:54:42.530
of the theory of fateishism.

00:54:42.530 --> 00:54:45.940
Marx stresses that fateishism

00:54:45.940 --> 00:54:50.940
is a reality commodities
have this feature.

00:54:51.710 --> 00:54:53.700
And why do they have this?

00:54:53.700 --> 00:54:56.150
Not as a result of a belief.

00:54:56.150 --> 00:55:01.150
No, they have this feature
because of a certain practice

00:55:02.900 --> 00:55:07.900
because of the way the
producers relate to each others.

00:55:08.680 --> 00:55:12.880
They relate not directly to each other.

00:55:12.880 --> 00:55:17.333
They relate mediated
through their products.

00:55:18.380 --> 00:55:23.090
And in so far, this Marx
stresses in "Capital",

00:55:23.090 --> 00:55:28.090
in the section, on fateishism,
the social properties

00:55:28.210 --> 00:55:33.210
of the producers and their
labor become social properties

00:55:34.630 --> 00:55:36.940
of their products.

00:55:36.940 --> 00:55:41.940
This is a necessity in
commodity production.

00:55:42.280 --> 00:55:45.690
The decisive, very short,

00:55:45.690 --> 00:55:48.720
but decisive explanation in "Capital"

00:55:48.720 --> 00:55:53.347
in penguin edition, you can
find in the pages 165, 66

00:55:57.330 --> 00:56:00.883
I just want to read one sentence.

00:56:01.990 --> 00:56:06.490
Now I quote, "The labor of the private

00:56:06.490 --> 00:56:11.490
individual manifests itself as
an element of the total labor

00:56:12.410 --> 00:56:17.410
of society, only through
the relations, which the act

00:56:18.720 --> 00:56:23.720
of exchange established
between the products

00:56:23.770 --> 00:56:26.973
and through the mediation
between the producers.

00:56:28.060 --> 00:56:33.060
To the producers, therefore,
the social relations

00:56:33.260 --> 00:56:37.810
between their private labors appear

00:56:37.810 --> 00:56:40.307
as what they are."

00:56:41.260 --> 00:56:44.830
This, "As what they
are," is very important.

00:56:44.830 --> 00:56:49.527
It says it is not wrong consciousness.

00:56:49.527 --> 00:56:51.883
"They appear as what they are.

00:56:52.830 --> 00:56:54.340
They do not appear

00:56:54.340 --> 00:56:58.450
as direct social relations
between persons in their work

00:56:59.780 --> 00:57:04.780
but rather as material
relations between persons

00:57:06.230 --> 00:57:11.230
and social relations between things."

00:57:13.670 --> 00:57:18.670
So I quoted this also
before it becomes clear

00:57:22.620 --> 00:57:27.620
that value theory for Marx
is not just the theory

00:57:27.810 --> 00:57:32.030
which explains us the exchange relations

00:57:32.030 --> 00:57:34.103
of different commodities,

00:57:35.080 --> 00:57:39.263
value theory in Marx is a social theory.

00:57:40.330 --> 00:57:45.330
It should explain how
in this rather perverse

00:57:46.400 --> 00:57:49.790
and I mean this literally,
in this perverse economy

00:57:51.380 --> 00:57:55.240
economy is possible, why it's perverse?

00:57:55.240 --> 00:57:59.430
You have, on the one hand
as social division of labor

00:57:59.430 --> 00:58:04.430
each producer is dependent
on a lot of other producers.

00:58:05.020 --> 00:58:08.800
There is a very strict social connection

00:58:08.800 --> 00:58:13.450
but the production is organized as

00:58:13.450 --> 00:58:18.450
if all producers were
Robinson's on a remote Ireland.

00:58:21.360 --> 00:58:24.683
They behave, the producers
behave atomistic.

00:58:25.620 --> 00:58:29.900
They do their decisions independently

00:58:29.900 --> 00:58:34.780
from all the other producers
and how this craziness

00:58:34.780 --> 00:58:39.780
that you have a connection, a
necessary material connection

00:58:41.020 --> 00:58:44.180
by all with the division of labor.

00:58:44.180 --> 00:58:46.110
And nevertheless you'll behave

00:58:46.110 --> 00:58:47.990
as if the others wouldn't exist.

00:58:47.990 --> 00:58:52.990
How this can work this special functioning

00:58:53.370 --> 00:58:58.370
of an economy should value theory explain

00:58:59.750 --> 00:59:03.770
in so far, it is a social theory.

00:59:03.770 --> 00:59:05.950
And in this social theory

00:59:05.950 --> 00:59:09.493
exchange plays a crucial role.

00:59:10.530 --> 00:59:15.530
There are a lot of opinions
who present Marx value theory

00:59:17.540 --> 00:59:22.340
determined by production already.

00:59:22.340 --> 00:59:25.720
These production oriented approaches

00:59:25.720 --> 00:59:29.370
miss the special character of value theory

00:59:29.370 --> 00:59:34.370
as a social theory, they also
miss the special character

00:59:35.360 --> 00:59:38.840
of a capitalist economy.

00:59:38.840 --> 00:59:43.840
So the question depends value
only on production, or depends

00:59:46.800 --> 00:59:51.800
on production and exchange is not

00:59:51.825 --> 00:59:56.825
a small theoretical question where

01:00:00.500 --> 01:00:05.280
you can write your PhD thesis about, but

01:00:05.280 --> 01:00:09.543
without much political impact.

01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:15.600
It, I think it is a very decisive question

01:00:18.250 --> 01:00:22.453
how to analyze, how to
understand capitalism.

01:00:25.670 --> 01:00:28.670
And here at this point

01:00:28.670 --> 01:00:33.560
I come to the next, very
related of the critical issues.

01:00:33.560 --> 01:00:34.443
It is money.

01:00:36.340 --> 01:00:41.150
When production already determines value

01:00:42.180 --> 01:00:45.800
then money is just the
expression of something

01:00:45.800 --> 01:00:49.300
what already exists before.

01:00:49.300 --> 01:00:50.363
Not very important.

01:00:52.000 --> 01:00:54.980
In Marx, it's rather different.

01:00:54.980 --> 01:00:57.310
In chapter three of "Capital"

01:00:57.310 --> 01:01:01.360
where Marx discusses the
functions of money he discussed

01:01:01.360 --> 01:01:04.803
as the first function measure of value.

01:01:06.430 --> 01:01:08.410
Marx there mentions

01:01:08.410 --> 01:01:13.410
that abstract labor is the
inner measure of value.

01:01:16.300 --> 01:01:19.140
Already in the first pages of "Capital"

01:01:19.140 --> 01:01:22.880
Marx stressed that value

01:01:22.880 --> 01:01:27.680
is across a crystal of abstract labor

01:01:29.040 --> 01:01:34.040
but now Marx introduces
money as measure of value.

01:01:35.130 --> 01:01:39.800
And not just as another
additional measure.

01:01:39.800 --> 01:01:44.800
No, he introduces money as the
only real measure of value.

01:01:46.470 --> 01:01:47.303
Why?

01:01:48.400 --> 01:01:49.833
Why he's doing this?

01:01:50.959 --> 01:01:54.300
Because abstract labor,

01:01:54.300 --> 01:01:57.380
as I try to explain before,

01:01:57.380 --> 01:02:00.270
you cannot find trust in the production

01:02:00.270 --> 01:02:03.650
when you measure labor with a clock

01:02:03.650 --> 01:02:06.110
you always measure the concrete

01:02:06.110 --> 01:02:09.733
individual labor of a certain worker.

01:02:11.070 --> 01:02:14.210
Abstract labor you can only find

01:02:14.210 --> 01:02:18.130
in exchange, but in exchange

01:02:18.130 --> 01:02:21.910
the commodities are
exchanged against money.

01:02:21.910 --> 01:02:24.330
At least when we have it to do with

01:02:24.330 --> 01:02:29.330
an developed exchange in
capitalist production.

01:02:30.300 --> 01:02:35.300
So money is the only
real measure of value.

01:02:37.470 --> 01:02:42.470
Abstract labor is, and
a strong analytical tool

01:02:44.800 --> 01:02:48.743
but you cannot use it for a real measure.

01:02:49.850 --> 01:02:54.850
Money as a measure of value
has its own properties.

01:02:56.340 --> 01:03:00.730
It can measure value correctly

01:03:00.730 --> 01:03:05.280
and it can measure value
in a not correct way,

01:03:05.280 --> 01:03:09.133
price and value can differ and even more,

01:03:11.520 --> 01:03:15.050
money can give a price to something

01:03:15.050 --> 01:03:16.990
which has no value.

01:03:16.990 --> 01:03:21.193
Marx stress this in chapter three.

01:03:23.690 --> 01:03:28.690
So when we now take these
critical issues together

01:03:33.360 --> 01:03:36.300
I think we can really speak

01:03:36.300 --> 01:03:40.470
of monetary value theory in Marx.

01:03:40.470 --> 01:03:44.910
It is not a production
oriented value theory.

01:03:44.910 --> 01:03:48.440
It is a monetary value theory

01:03:52.890 --> 01:03:57.890
where we however, have
also to regard that money

01:03:58.490 --> 01:04:01.870
in this theory is not just a simple thing

01:04:01.870 --> 01:04:05.380
as it is very often regarded.

01:04:05.380 --> 01:04:09.840
I just remind you the
steps of analysis of money

01:04:09.840 --> 01:04:13.670
at the beginning of "Capital"
in chapter one necessity

01:04:13.670 --> 01:04:15.300
of the money form.

01:04:15.300 --> 01:04:19.450
Chapter two, commodity owners act

01:04:19.450 --> 01:04:24.450
as character masks of
personifications and forced to sought

01:04:24.850 --> 01:04:28.730
out real money, a commodity as real money.

01:04:28.730 --> 01:04:29.810
And chapter three,

01:04:29.810 --> 01:04:34.810
the money functions, not
as an arbitrary enumeration

01:04:34.930 --> 01:04:39.633
but derived from commodity circulation.

01:04:40.760 --> 01:04:42.500
And this is not the last step

01:04:42.500 --> 01:04:44.230
in the theory of money.

01:04:44.230 --> 01:04:47.600
In volume three regarding credit

01:04:47.600 --> 01:04:52.600
we will find credit money and
fictitious capital and so on.

01:04:53.440 --> 01:04:58.440
So money itself is a complicated
thing which plays a role

01:05:01.894 --> 01:05:06.894
or which is developed for
all volumes of capital.

01:05:09.860 --> 01:05:14.520
So I said, taking all
these critical issues

01:05:14.520 --> 01:05:19.010
I try to present you briefly together

01:05:19.010 --> 01:05:24.010
we can say Marx value
theory is a monetary theory

01:05:26.310 --> 01:05:31.210
which needs production
and exchange production

01:05:31.210 --> 01:05:34.400
and circulation to explain something.

01:05:34.400 --> 01:05:37.280
And this story of value theory continues.

01:05:37.280 --> 01:05:41.030
It's not at the end, in the third chapter

01:05:41.030 --> 01:05:46.030
it continues with capital,
not the book "Capital"

01:05:47.367 --> 01:05:50.270
the thing and the category capital.

01:05:50.270 --> 01:05:53.870
Also here, we can say Marx presents

01:05:53.870 --> 01:05:56.923
a monetary capital theory.

01:05:59.260 --> 01:06:04.260
Already in chapter four,
when Marx analyzes the

01:06:04.880 --> 01:06:09.880
circulation figure MCM dash
money commodity more money.

01:06:13.620 --> 01:06:18.240
He starts the discussing
the formed determination

01:06:18.240 --> 01:06:21.100
of capital with money, and he stresses

01:06:21.100 --> 01:06:26.100
that capital needs money as
an independent expression

01:06:27.020 --> 01:06:30.770
of capital only in the using capital

01:06:30.770 --> 01:06:35.770
you can also do all the
accountings later with profit rate

01:06:37.200 --> 01:06:38.460
as a measure

01:06:38.460 --> 01:06:40.803
for valorization and so on.

01:06:42.150 --> 01:06:45.550
Production and exploitation come

01:06:45.550 --> 01:06:49.657
into the game already in a chapter five

01:06:52.010 --> 01:06:57.010
and six, they shall
explain why valorization

01:06:58.380 --> 01:07:03.360
which we found in the formed
analysis, is possible.

01:07:03.360 --> 01:07:08.360
It rests on exploitation,
but you cannot reduce

01:07:09.120 --> 01:07:12.550
capital just on exploitation.

01:07:12.550 --> 01:07:15.880
Exploitation you have
in every class society

01:07:16.840 --> 01:07:21.840
in a society or in an
economy based on slave work

01:07:21.860 --> 01:07:25.640
or in a society like in

01:07:25.640 --> 01:07:28.576
medieval times on unfree work

01:07:28.576 --> 01:07:32.600
and so on everywhere
you have exploitation.

01:07:32.600 --> 01:07:36.550
But to understand the specific

01:07:36.550 --> 01:07:41.240
capitalists structure of
exploitation, you need money

01:07:42.480 --> 01:07:47.480
and Marx himself stresses this

01:07:47.630 --> 01:07:51.400
in at the end of the fifth chapter.

01:07:51.400 --> 01:07:54.950
Let me just read one short quote

01:07:57.070 --> 01:08:01.713
which you can find in penguin
edition on the page 268

01:08:06.200 --> 01:08:10.900
where first Marx describe
the contradiction in

01:08:13.340 --> 01:08:17.200
attempts to explain capital.

01:08:17.200 --> 01:08:19.433
So Marx concluded now the quote,

01:08:20.437 --> 01:08:25.437
"Capital cannot therefore
arise from circulation

01:08:27.470 --> 01:08:29.640
and it is equally impossible for

01:08:29.640 --> 01:08:33.643
it to arise apart from circulation.

01:08:34.760 --> 01:08:36.870
It must have its origin

01:08:36.870 --> 01:08:41.870
both in circulation and
not in circulation."

01:08:45.290 --> 01:08:50.290
So also capital is not
something which you can reduce

01:08:51.800 --> 01:08:53.610
to production like value.

01:08:53.610 --> 01:08:57.900
You need production and circulation.

01:08:57.900 --> 01:09:02.160
And now when you look on the
structure of the three volumes

01:09:02.160 --> 01:09:07.160
of "Capital", volume one
focuses on production

01:09:09.760 --> 01:09:14.760
after the first five chapters,
and many people think,

01:09:18.867 --> 01:09:23.867
"Oh, this is the core of
Marx analysis of capitalism."

01:09:23.870 --> 01:09:27.980
No, the whole is the core.

01:09:27.980 --> 01:09:29.880
You need the second volume

01:09:29.880 --> 01:09:33.580
on circulation, and you
need the third volume

01:09:33.580 --> 01:09:38.580
of the process as a whole, where profit

01:09:40.560 --> 01:09:44.000
and interest is introduced
such important categories.

01:09:44.000 --> 01:09:47.100
And from these two further volumes

01:09:47.100 --> 01:09:48.810
the second and the third volume

01:09:48.810 --> 01:09:53.810
you also can learn that
you cannot make a division

01:09:56.520 --> 01:09:59.310
which is very popular between a

01:09:59.310 --> 01:10:04.310
so-called real capital and a speculative

01:10:04.500 --> 01:10:09.500
financial area as if this
were two different worlds.

01:10:10.760 --> 01:10:15.140
No, the same way as you cannot have

01:10:15.140 --> 01:10:20.140
a pure value sphere apart
from any money relations

01:10:22.240 --> 01:10:26.790
you can have a pure capitalist real

01:10:26.790 --> 01:10:31.343
production sphere apart
from any speculation,

01:10:32.440 --> 01:10:37.440
money market, credit, interest
fearing capital and so on.

01:10:37.940 --> 01:10:41.280
This is very important for

01:10:41.280 --> 01:10:44.690
understanding contemporary capitalism.

01:10:44.690 --> 01:10:47.060
And of course it is very important

01:10:47.060 --> 01:10:49.837
when we want to fight against capitalism.

01:10:49.837 --> 01:10:53.370
When we want to overcome capitalism

01:10:53.370 --> 01:10:58.370
we must really understand
capitalism as a whole.

01:10:59.770 --> 01:11:03.350
However, my time is at the end

01:11:03.350 --> 01:11:06.670
I had to speak here about value theory.

01:11:06.670 --> 01:11:09.070
So understanding capitalism as a

01:11:09.070 --> 01:11:14.070
whole is another story I will
finish here at this point.

01:11:14.310 --> 01:11:16.370
And thank you very much

01:11:16.370 --> 01:11:19.300
for your patience and for your attention.

01:11:19.300 --> 01:11:20.363
Thank you very much.

01:11:21.400 --> 01:11:22.410
- Thank you Michael.

01:11:22.410 --> 01:11:25.050
This is the place where there
would be thunderous applause.

01:11:25.050 --> 01:11:28.690
I like to mark the place, what
we're missing in this world.

01:11:28.690 --> 01:11:29.790
So imagine it's there.

01:11:29.790 --> 01:11:30.623
I should, you know

01:11:30.623 --> 01:11:34.300
they make us into these TV,
these unwilling TV celebrities.

01:11:34.300 --> 01:11:36.870
We should at least have an applause track

01:11:36.870 --> 01:11:40.603
I'm gonna ask for that next
time, maybe not a laugh track.

01:11:41.615 --> 01:11:42.840
And so thank you very much.

01:11:42.840 --> 01:11:47.840
I want to ask people to put questions

01:11:48.380 --> 01:11:51.660
in the chat, which they're already doing.

01:11:51.660 --> 01:11:53.630
I wanna start out with one question to you

01:11:53.630 --> 01:11:58.190
if you don't mind, it's going
back to the relationship

01:11:58.190 --> 01:12:03.190
between money and socially
necessary, average labor time.

01:12:05.480 --> 01:12:06.380
If you don't mind.

01:12:07.630 --> 01:12:10.070
I just want to hear a little
bit more about this from you.

01:12:10.070 --> 01:12:10.903
I've read a lot

01:12:10.903 --> 01:12:13.040
about it from you, but I think
it's good for people to hear.

01:12:13.040 --> 01:12:17.990
I'm thinking of the first
page of chapter three

01:12:19.260 --> 01:12:22.380
where he says at the end,
I'm gonna read the penguin

01:12:22.380 --> 01:12:24.940
which we are working with my colleague

01:12:24.940 --> 01:12:26.670
Paul Reitter to update

01:12:26.670 --> 01:12:28.190
in a new translation

01:12:28.190 --> 01:12:31.350
but they say in the penguin,
"Money as a measure of value

01:12:31.350 --> 01:12:34.280
as the necessary form of
appearance of the measure of value

01:12:34.280 --> 01:12:37.767
which is imminent of the
commodities, namely labor time."

01:12:39.500 --> 01:12:42.270
So I can understand you saying

01:12:42.270 --> 01:12:45.950
that, bringing money
back into the equation,

01:12:45.950 --> 01:12:50.830
let's say, and changing the
revolutionary possibilities

01:12:50.830 --> 01:12:53.113
of the book by doing that.

01:12:58.965 --> 01:13:03.477
- Okay, the last remark is a
little bit too pathetic to say,

01:13:03.477 --> 01:13:06.970
"Oh, now I changed everything".

01:13:06.970 --> 01:13:08.230
- Well you don't change everything.

01:13:08.230 --> 01:13:12.770
But you add, I mean, it isn't that well

01:13:12.770 --> 01:13:15.120
that's a long discussion
anyway, that's my hope

01:13:17.890 --> 01:13:21.590
But still he has to call
it a form of appearance.

01:13:21.590 --> 01:13:22.810
So I wanna know if you could

01:13:22.810 --> 01:13:24.590
say more than what role does the

01:13:24.590 --> 01:13:28.610
immanent form have socially
necessary labor time

01:13:29.630 --> 01:13:32.640
in the value form.

01:13:32.640 --> 01:13:34.010
Money measures it practically.

01:13:34.010 --> 01:13:36.510
I know that I'm gonna
measure value in money.

01:13:36.510 --> 01:13:39.920
We know this, what you don't
know, what's being shown

01:13:39.920 --> 01:13:42.710
by Marx is that you're
actually measuring it in

01:13:42.710 --> 01:13:45.200
in socially necessary labor time

01:13:45.200 --> 01:13:47.290
which is a huge innovation,

01:13:47.290 --> 01:13:51.810
it seems to me, over the
classical political economists

01:13:51.810 --> 01:13:53.270
and over the, let's say a kind

01:13:53.270 --> 01:13:54.903
of empirical view of
the economy, which says

01:13:54.903 --> 01:13:58.510
that money is the money
is the root of everything.

01:13:58.510 --> 01:13:59.700
It's really about money.

01:13:59.700 --> 01:14:04.180
Here it's about how people's efforts,

01:14:04.180 --> 01:14:06.270
social efforts to make something

01:14:06.270 --> 01:14:08.290
are valued when they're put together

01:14:08.290 --> 01:14:11.010
with other social efforts to make things

01:14:11.010 --> 01:14:12.090
in the exchange realm.

01:14:12.090 --> 01:14:13.850
So I agree completely that it's

01:14:13.850 --> 01:14:16.740
between production and
exchange, that value arises

01:14:19.530 --> 01:14:22.050
but what role does this
imminent form play?

01:14:22.050 --> 01:14:23.100
And why should the form

01:14:23.100 --> 01:14:26.253
of appearance be given
such pride of place?

01:14:28.620 --> 01:14:32.050
- This eminent form, this eminent measure

01:14:32.050 --> 01:14:37.050
of value plays the crucial
role in understanding value.

01:14:41.358 --> 01:14:46.358
Value is a certain kind of
objectivity, now we came

01:14:48.310 --> 01:14:51.080
to the translation problems in German.

01:14:51.080 --> 01:14:52.750
I would say, (speaks in foreign language).

01:14:53.660 --> 01:14:55.860
A certain time of, (speaks
in foreign language),

01:14:58.930 --> 01:15:00.610
thing like ness.

01:15:00.610 --> 01:15:04.370
When I allow to construct an English word

01:15:06.064 --> 01:15:08.150
a certain kind of objectivity

01:15:08.150 --> 01:15:13.150
which on the one hand has features

01:15:13.310 --> 01:15:18.310
of physical objectivity of
analog to in an analog way

01:15:19.610 --> 01:15:24.610
to physical objectivity,
but it is not physical.

01:15:26.760 --> 01:15:30.830
And this special kind of objectivity

01:15:30.830 --> 01:15:35.830
I think is one of the
main discoveries of Marx.

01:15:37.870 --> 01:15:42.870
When you look to the
history of political economy

01:15:43.550 --> 01:15:48.550
there is a certain dispute
is economy a science,

01:15:51.390 --> 01:15:52.820
a natural science

01:15:52.820 --> 01:15:55.140
with physical quantities?

01:15:55.140 --> 01:15:58.970
Or is it a kind of psychology

01:15:58.970 --> 01:16:03.940
because it deals with consumer
preferences and so on.

01:16:03.940 --> 01:16:08.690
And the discussions are
sometimes very stupid

01:16:08.690 --> 01:16:10.270
when you read this.

01:16:10.270 --> 01:16:15.270
Marx, I think exactly in his
value theory can show value is

01:16:15.330 --> 01:16:19.753
neither the one nor the
other, neither it has to do

01:16:21.939 --> 01:16:23.960
with physical quantities.

01:16:23.960 --> 01:16:26.360
It is not like a natural science

01:16:26.360 --> 01:16:28.923
but it is also not a kind of psychology.

01:16:29.760 --> 01:16:34.710
And to understand this special objectivity

01:16:35.940 --> 01:16:39.280
you need the term abstract labor

01:16:39.280 --> 01:16:43.680
the difference concrete
and abstract labor.

01:16:43.680 --> 01:16:48.680
So it is a necessary
step in the appropriation

01:16:53.800 --> 01:16:58.800
of our object of research
in the introduction

01:16:59.140 --> 01:17:04.140
of 1857 Marx made this
nice remark relating

01:17:06.100 --> 01:17:11.070
to Hagle that we must appropriate

01:17:11.070 --> 01:17:16.070
in our thoughts, the objective thing

01:17:16.240 --> 01:17:20.760
we do research and this we
can do only with categories.

01:17:20.760 --> 01:17:23.860
And so abstract labor is one

01:17:23.860 --> 01:17:27.770
of the decisive categories
for this appropriation

01:17:27.770 --> 01:17:31.900
but it is not at all a
category you can measure.

01:17:31.900 --> 01:17:36.810
You can, you can say 10
hours abstract labor.

01:17:36.810 --> 01:17:40.520
This term makes absolutely no sense.

01:17:40.520 --> 01:17:42.360
Here you need money.

01:17:42.360 --> 01:17:43.380
And when you talk

01:17:43.380 --> 01:17:46.570
about, or Marx talks about a shyness form,

01:17:46.570 --> 01:17:51.570
a form of appearance, we also
have to be very cautious.

01:17:52.800 --> 01:17:55.190
Many people know this term

01:17:55.190 --> 01:17:58.190
maybe coming from a Galilean philosophy

01:17:58.190 --> 01:18:01.930
there is an essence, and
there is a form of appearance.

01:18:01.930 --> 01:18:06.550
And many people think, ah
essence is the essence.

01:18:06.550 --> 01:18:07.900
The important part

01:18:07.900 --> 01:18:12.610
and form of appearance is
something hanging around

01:18:12.610 --> 01:18:16.410
not so important, but
this is totally wrong

01:18:16.410 --> 01:18:17.977
already with Hagle

01:18:19.700 --> 01:18:23.880
form of appearance is
absolutely necessary.

01:18:23.880 --> 01:18:27.210
And in Hagle not to

01:18:29.010 --> 01:18:34.010
put apart from essence and
now read "Capital" volume one.

01:18:34.360 --> 01:18:37.370
Where do you find the term essence?

01:18:37.370 --> 01:18:40.780
Not in the first few
chapters, you have form

01:18:40.780 --> 01:18:43.640
of appearance, but without essence.

01:18:43.640 --> 01:18:45.700
So this means also form

01:18:45.700 --> 01:18:50.560
of appearance is something
different from what Hagle means.

01:18:50.560 --> 01:18:53.420
And also what many contemporary

01:18:53.420 --> 01:18:55.820
Marxists understand this form

01:18:55.820 --> 01:18:59.630
of appearance, but for our
discussion, it is important.

01:18:59.630 --> 01:19:02.253
It is not any subordinated.

01:19:05.440 --> 01:19:10.230
So I hope I could give some
ideas to your question.

01:19:10.230 --> 01:19:11.710
- Many, many ideas.

01:19:11.710 --> 01:19:13.060
I thank you for it.

01:19:13.060 --> 01:19:16.150
We have a lot of questions in the chat.

01:19:16.150 --> 01:19:17.330
So what I'm going to do,

01:19:17.330 --> 01:19:19.410
let's see how many do
we have, maybe about 10.

01:19:19.410 --> 01:19:21.580
I think we'll just do
it the way we usually do

01:19:21.580 --> 01:19:24.550
which is we'll start with the first one.

01:19:24.550 --> 01:19:27.550
- Yeah, hi thanks Michael,
for this interesting talk

01:19:27.550 --> 01:19:29.610
I just had a quick
question about your claim

01:19:29.610 --> 01:19:31.820
that Marx begins the "Capital" volume one

01:19:31.820 --> 01:19:35.303
with form determination
instead of human action.

01:19:36.330 --> 01:19:39.950
But I'm very interested
myself in those opening pages

01:19:39.950 --> 01:19:43.210
and the way that Marx
says that a use value

01:19:43.210 --> 01:19:47.150
to a consumer is a necessary
condition for a commodity.

01:19:47.150 --> 01:19:49.730
Otherwise, an object won't be exchanged

01:19:49.730 --> 01:19:51.710
and it can't have exchange value at all.

01:19:51.710 --> 01:19:53.703
And so it can't even be a commodity.

01:19:54.630 --> 01:19:55.730
So with that in mind,

01:19:55.730 --> 01:19:58.300
can't we say that commodities always begin

01:19:58.300 --> 01:20:00.650
with human action and
therefore Marx account

01:20:00.650 --> 01:20:03.990
of commodities, even though
he's obviously concerned

01:20:03.990 --> 01:20:07.763
with form determination also
begins with human action?

01:20:10.570 --> 01:20:11.820
- Okay, why don't we take one more?

01:20:11.820 --> 01:20:12.910
Does that sound right?

01:20:12.910 --> 01:20:13.890
Thank you Tristan.

01:20:13.890 --> 01:20:16.343
Let's open up Michael Pulsford.

01:20:17.950 --> 01:20:19.150
Michael, you'll be next.

01:20:30.180 --> 01:20:32.210
Or if we don't get to Michael
or if Michael has left

01:20:32.210 --> 01:20:33.400
I can read the question.

01:20:33.400 --> 01:20:34.233
That's fine.

01:20:38.660 --> 01:20:42.500
- Not think that they are still on.

01:20:42.500 --> 01:20:43.810
- Well let's ask the question anyway,

01:20:43.810 --> 01:20:45.900
for the sake of fairness.

01:20:45.900 --> 01:20:47.030
I'll go ahead and do it.

01:20:47.030 --> 01:20:48.027
This is from Michael.

01:20:48.027 --> 01:20:51.140
"Did Marx say anything
about marginal theories

01:20:51.140 --> 01:20:53.940
of value and what do you think
he might've said about value

01:20:53.940 --> 01:20:55.120
if he was writing a critique

01:20:55.120 --> 01:20:57.500
of political economy, a few decades later

01:20:57.500 --> 01:21:00.380
and marginal theories
of value were dominant?"

01:21:00.380 --> 01:21:01.460
This is a great question.

01:21:01.460 --> 01:21:02.590
I would have asked it myself.

01:21:02.590 --> 01:21:05.053
So Michael, if you're
listening, thank you.

01:21:05.970 --> 01:21:08.313
Okay Michael, I think you're on.

01:21:10.360 --> 01:21:11.763
- Okay, shall I answer?

01:21:12.610 --> 01:21:13.443
Okay.

01:21:15.870 --> 01:21:17.920
Interesting questions

01:21:17.920 --> 01:21:21.873
maybe we will have a long evening.

01:21:24.030 --> 01:21:29.030
I will start with the
second question of Tristan.

01:21:32.720 --> 01:21:37.720
You mentioned that Marx
in the first sub section

01:21:38.290 --> 01:21:41.850
of the first chapter already stressed

01:21:42.690 --> 01:21:45.830
every commodity needs use value

01:21:45.830 --> 01:21:49.530
otherwise it cannot be a commodity.

01:21:49.530 --> 01:21:54.530
And if this not means that he
also starts with human action

01:21:54.830 --> 01:21:57.048
then I understood correctly.

01:21:57.048 --> 01:22:01.560
I think Marx mentioned,
it is totally correct

01:22:01.560 --> 01:22:03.070
what you said about Marx,

01:22:03.070 --> 01:22:08.070
he mentioned this as a kind
of general prerequisite.

01:22:09.700 --> 01:22:11.790
It must have use value and it must

01:22:11.790 --> 01:22:14.050
have use value for others.

01:22:14.050 --> 01:22:17.380
Not only for me then
it's also not a commodity

01:22:17.380 --> 01:22:21.910
but this general prerequisite doesn't

01:22:21.910 --> 01:22:26.910
constitute a certain approach
on which he's building.

01:22:26.970 --> 01:22:28.630
He also could say

01:22:30.540 --> 01:22:34.430
when we have commodity
production and exchange

01:22:34.430 --> 01:22:36.190
it is done by humans.

01:22:36.190 --> 01:22:39.090
Humans have to breathe, without breathing

01:22:39.090 --> 01:22:43.090
there is no commodity
production, but does this mean

01:22:43.090 --> 01:22:47.270
that Marx would have
presented a biologist approach

01:22:48.702 --> 01:22:53.450
when just stating people
always have to breathe

01:22:53.450 --> 01:22:56.530
it would only give a very,

01:22:56.530 --> 01:23:00.553
very general prerequisite.

01:23:01.482 --> 01:23:06.482
And in such way, I would
interpret the, what you mentioned.

01:23:08.370 --> 01:23:11.943
Marx says okay, it must have use value,

01:23:13.010 --> 01:23:18.010
but now let's analyze the economic forms.

01:23:18.960 --> 01:23:23.950
And the action comes then
in the second chapter

01:23:23.950 --> 01:23:28.300
which already starts
with this nice sentence

01:23:28.300 --> 01:23:32.460
the commodities cannot
go alone to the market.

01:23:32.460 --> 01:23:37.380
Therefore we have to
look for their owners.

01:23:37.380 --> 01:23:40.070
So also here Marx makes clear

01:23:40.070 --> 01:23:42.480
now we change perspective.

01:23:42.480 --> 01:23:47.480
Now first we really come to
the persons and the actions.

01:23:52.370 --> 01:23:57.370
The third question about marginal theory

01:23:58.070 --> 01:24:02.080
is this is really an interesting point.

01:24:02.080 --> 01:24:07.080
There were some very rough predecessors

01:24:08.150 --> 01:24:13.150
in British economy who
also tried not make use

01:24:13.250 --> 01:24:16.910
of marginal theory, but of utility theory

01:24:16.910 --> 01:24:21.910
Marx knew them and counted
them as vulgar economics.

01:24:23.990 --> 01:24:28.603
But I found no hint that he ever saw

01:24:32.350 --> 01:24:34.040
the book of Jevons.

01:24:34.040 --> 01:24:35.660
The book of Jevons was published

01:24:35.660 --> 01:24:40.473
in London in the year 1871 or 72.

01:24:42.130 --> 01:24:44.380
A time by Marx was in London

01:24:44.380 --> 01:24:48.330
where he followed economic discussions

01:24:48.330 --> 01:24:53.330
neither in a letter, neither in a draft,

01:24:54.070 --> 01:24:59.070
in a notebook, nowhere
this book or another book

01:24:59.220 --> 01:25:03.743
of marginalist literature is mentioned.

01:25:04.920 --> 01:25:09.920
So far, and I would expect to
find at least a short remark

01:25:13.250 --> 01:25:17.000
what nonsense nowadays
produced or something,

01:25:17.000 --> 01:25:19.270
but no, nothing.

01:25:19.270 --> 01:25:22.410
Another book of Jevons.

01:25:22.410 --> 01:25:24.310
He knew about the coal production

01:25:24.310 --> 01:25:27.140
the coal industry, a book,
which appeared earlier.

01:25:27.140 --> 01:25:30.183
So he also knew the
name, but not this book.

01:25:31.600 --> 01:25:33.240
And in so far

01:25:33.240 --> 01:25:38.240
I think he missed the rise
of this marginalist theory.

01:25:39.460 --> 01:25:44.300
He was in 70, 71, he was very occupied

01:25:44.300 --> 01:25:48.060
with the civil war in France
to write civil war in France

01:25:48.060 --> 01:25:53.060
with the Paris comun.

01:25:53.330 --> 01:25:55.320
Then he was sick.

01:25:55.320 --> 01:26:00.320
Then he was trying to finish
volume two of "Capital".

01:26:02.460 --> 01:26:07.460
And this marginalism, he
missed this marginalism.

01:26:08.500 --> 01:26:12.440
And when he couldn't miss
it, because it was so broad

01:26:12.440 --> 01:26:15.253
in the 80s, he was dead.

01:26:16.380 --> 01:26:19.410
So he didn't see it.

01:26:19.410 --> 01:26:23.080
And you asked also what would be his

01:26:23.080 --> 01:26:26.450
estimation, difficult question.

01:26:26.450 --> 01:26:31.410
I would say, I would suppose his

01:26:31.410 --> 01:26:36.410
answer to it would have two
paths, stressing utility.

01:26:38.020 --> 01:26:41.310
He would criticize very heavily.

01:26:41.310 --> 01:26:44.800
To introduce mathematical formalism

01:26:44.800 --> 01:26:49.800
in, especially the differential calculus

01:26:50.000 --> 01:26:51.410
he would appreciate.

01:26:51.410 --> 01:26:56.410
He himself did intensive
study of differential calculus

01:26:57.270 --> 01:26:59.370
since mid of the 60s

01:26:59.370 --> 01:27:03.780
there are mathematical
notebooks, only one is published

01:27:03.780 --> 01:27:07.290
until now, but there are
more mathematical notebooks

01:27:07.290 --> 01:27:11.410
which will be published in mega.

01:27:11.410 --> 01:27:15.310
And from this, you'll see
that he really was engaged

01:27:17.410 --> 01:27:20.640
occupying knowledge, not
he was not a researcher

01:27:20.640 --> 01:27:25.640
a mathematical researcher, but
he was a person who tried to

01:27:25.970 --> 01:27:28.343
learn all this calculus.

01:27:30.110 --> 01:27:34.793
So now the first question,
which has several parts

01:27:39.260 --> 01:27:43.330
and which not, I think not all parts

01:27:43.330 --> 01:27:48.330
are so narrow to my talk.

01:27:49.900 --> 01:27:53.457
One part is you mentioned pessimists

01:27:56.250 --> 01:28:00.680
Adornian outlook, or Adornian theories.

01:28:00.680 --> 01:28:05.240
And I think I also
mentioned this in Brighton.

01:28:05.240 --> 01:28:08.270
Some people, some pupils

01:28:08.270 --> 01:28:13.270
of Adorno understand
fateishism as a closed system

01:28:15.930 --> 01:28:20.930
you cannot, you cannot
escape from fateishism.

01:28:24.030 --> 01:28:25.713
It is everywhere.

01:28:26.910 --> 01:28:28.370
If this would be true

01:28:29.500 --> 01:28:34.500
then the question is, how
can we talk about fateishism?

01:28:35.230 --> 01:28:38.080
When we are such imprisoned by fateishism

01:28:38.080 --> 01:28:39.660
how can we analyze it?

01:28:39.660 --> 01:28:44.660
So this totally closed world
by fateishism cannot exist.

01:28:47.820 --> 01:28:50.770
Otherwise we couldn't talk about

01:28:50.770 --> 01:28:53.730
and I would say fateishism is an

01:28:53.730 --> 01:28:58.730
always present background
for workers, for capitalists,

01:28:59.820 --> 01:29:02.240
for academics non academics,

01:29:02.240 --> 01:29:04.830
and we cannot escape this background

01:29:04.830 --> 01:29:07.510
but how we react to this background

01:29:07.510 --> 01:29:10.290
what this background is doing with us

01:29:10.290 --> 01:29:15.290
has to do with our practice,
our practical life as well

01:29:15.810 --> 01:29:19.350
with our knowledge with
our intellectual life

01:29:19.350 --> 01:29:21.110
what we do learn.

01:29:21.110 --> 01:29:23.170
And here I would say

01:29:23.170 --> 01:29:28.170
at least with these
extreme Adornian pupils

01:29:28.410 --> 01:29:31.163
I have big differences.

01:29:32.130 --> 01:29:37.130
You also mentioned pessimistic outlooks.

01:29:37.340 --> 01:29:39.611
This is true in Adorno.

01:29:39.611 --> 01:29:42.400
And in, especially I think even

01:29:42.400 --> 01:29:46.913
more in Horkheimer you
can find such a pessimism.

01:29:49.210 --> 01:29:54.210
Also this, I wouldn't
share, Adorno and Horkheimer

01:29:56.900 --> 01:30:00.050
they had the experience of fascism.

01:30:00.050 --> 01:30:05.050
They had the experience
of nearly break down

01:30:06.160 --> 01:30:11.160
of human society by fascism
and by the second world war.

01:30:11.200 --> 01:30:14.780
And they had reason to be pessimistic

01:30:14.780 --> 01:30:18.500
but in analytical terms

01:30:18.500 --> 01:30:23.240
I think there is not such a good reason.

01:30:23.240 --> 01:30:27.913
And when Adorno and
Horkheimer saw the hints

01:30:29.610 --> 01:30:32.340
that they, they have not to be so

01:30:32.340 --> 01:30:36.030
pessimistic when the
students movement emerged

01:30:36.030 --> 01:30:38.680
they had big problems with
the student movements.

01:30:38.680 --> 01:30:40.830
I think also because they were

01:30:40.830 --> 01:30:43.763
in a pessimistic way prejudiced.

01:30:44.810 --> 01:30:48.400
Nowadays, I think we also have reason

01:30:48.400 --> 01:30:53.400
to some pessimism because a
totally different situation.

01:30:55.460 --> 01:30:58.760
Capitalism is destroying so much

01:30:58.760 --> 01:31:03.610
the planet that perhaps the capitalism

01:31:03.610 --> 01:31:05.380
will have destroyed the planet

01:31:05.380 --> 01:31:07.610
and our foundations

01:31:07.610 --> 01:31:12.610
of living before we can
succeed to destroy capitalism.

01:31:13.150 --> 01:31:16.320
It is a competition in time.

01:31:16.320 --> 01:31:17.260
And I must say

01:31:17.260 --> 01:31:21.880
I'm not sure if we will
win this competition.

01:31:21.880 --> 01:31:26.610
So this could be a pessimist, also a kind

01:31:26.610 --> 01:31:29.470
of pessimism, but this
could be totally different

01:31:29.470 --> 01:31:34.070
from Adornian or Horkheimer pessimism

01:31:34.070 --> 01:31:37.673
but also in this case,
I still have some hope.

01:31:39.810 --> 01:31:41.700
- Okay, thank you.

01:31:41.700 --> 01:31:43.730
We're going to do a few more questions.

01:31:43.730 --> 01:31:47.070
We'll start with my
colleague, Paul Reitter, Paul?

01:31:47.070 --> 01:31:49.870
- Michael, thank you so much

01:31:49.870 --> 01:31:52.500
for your talk and for being here.

01:31:52.500 --> 01:31:53.880
I can't remember

01:31:53.880 --> 01:31:55.323
whether or not Paul
said this, but you know

01:31:55.323 --> 01:31:57.210
Michael is in Berlin right now.

01:31:57.210 --> 01:31:59.380
So it's 1:30 in the morning

01:31:59.380 --> 01:32:01.988
and you know, the energy and lucidity

01:32:01.988 --> 01:32:04.973
at this advanced hour
really are quite stunning.

01:32:06.147 --> 01:32:11.147
So I admire that and much
else about this presentation.

01:32:11.349 --> 01:32:14.440
I want to ask a question
that I've asked before.

01:32:14.440 --> 01:32:18.130
And so I forgive people in the
audience who are maybe tired

01:32:18.130 --> 01:32:21.340
of this question by now, but
this time is a little different

01:32:21.340 --> 01:32:24.210
because, whereas I don't
think the question has gotten

01:32:24.210 --> 01:32:27.093
that much traction with
previous presenters,

01:32:28.360 --> 01:32:29.580
you actually have spoken

01:32:29.580 --> 01:32:33.440
to this issue a bit and seem
interested in taking it up.

01:32:33.440 --> 01:32:35.367
And the issue is what it is

01:32:35.367 --> 01:32:39.040
on the level of, on the level of rhetoric.

01:32:39.040 --> 01:32:39.873
How is it

01:32:39.873 --> 01:32:44.150
that this text lends itself
to misreadings and you talked

01:32:44.150 --> 01:32:49.150
about the popularizing
formulations in the section

01:32:50.560 --> 01:32:54.870
on value that you think
are a bit misleading.

01:32:54.870 --> 01:32:57.110
And I would be really interested to hear

01:32:57.110 --> 01:32:58.823
some examples of that.

01:33:00.710 --> 01:33:02.470
- Of the misreadings?

01:33:02.470 --> 01:33:04.436
- Or of the popularizing formulations

01:33:04.436 --> 01:33:09.436
in the 1872 edition that lend themselves

01:33:09.868 --> 01:33:12.653
to misreadings misinterpretation.

01:33:13.540 --> 01:33:15.380
- [Michael] Okay.

01:33:15.380 --> 01:33:17.580
- Michael I'll read one

01:33:17.580 --> 01:33:19.600
that from the chat, just to add

01:33:19.600 --> 01:33:24.563
to your list, this is
from Anne Lee Summers.

01:33:27.894 --> 01:33:28.817
She asked me to ask the question,

01:33:28.817 --> 01:33:31.530
"Could professor Heinrich
comment on the usefulness

01:33:31.530 --> 01:33:34.930
of studying book four of
"Capital" namely theories

01:33:34.930 --> 01:33:38.660
of surplus value in the
context of a critical reading

01:33:38.660 --> 01:33:40.730
of 'Capital' volumes, one, two, and three

01:33:40.730 --> 01:33:42.890
you seem to emphasize those three

01:33:42.890 --> 01:33:47.640
but not so much the
supposed or potential four.

01:33:47.640 --> 01:33:51.040
Also, could you comment on
the accuracy or usefulness

01:33:51.040 --> 01:33:54.480
of Alan Oakley's 1983
bibliographic analysis

01:33:54.480 --> 01:33:55.997
of Marx's critical theory?"

01:33:57.070 --> 01:33:59.540
So that's two questions
from Anne Lee Summers.

01:33:59.540 --> 01:34:01.303
And shall we give you one more?

01:34:02.666 --> 01:34:06.020
- Alan Oakley's what I,
I didn't understand that-

01:34:06.020 --> 01:34:10.660
- 1983 bibliographic analysis
of Marx's critical theory.

01:34:10.660 --> 01:34:12.810
I don't know that myself, but.

01:34:12.810 --> 01:34:13.643
- Yeah

01:34:13.643 --> 01:34:18.000
I know the name of Alan Oakley,
but this book, I don't know.

01:34:18.000 --> 01:34:20.670
I have nothing to say.

01:34:20.670 --> 01:34:22.290
- Okay.

01:34:22.290 --> 01:34:24.670
Now-
- I will say later.

01:34:24.670 --> 01:34:28.210
- Okay, shall we have one-
- Do another one.

01:34:28.210 --> 01:34:32.233
- Okay, so Audrey, could
you open Allen Sbanotto?

01:34:36.600 --> 01:34:37.873
- [Allen] Hi, can hear me?

01:34:38.860 --> 01:34:40.470
- Yes, we hear you fine.

01:34:40.470 --> 01:34:41.470
- [Allen] All right.

01:34:41.470 --> 01:34:43.610
I hope this isn't too narrow or technical

01:34:43.610 --> 01:34:47.770
but I just wanted to ask,
you had mentioned the value

01:34:47.770 --> 01:34:50.010
of a commodity can differ from its price.

01:34:50.010 --> 01:34:51.990
And that makes sense.

01:34:51.990 --> 01:34:54.230
There's all kinds of
reasons why that can happen

01:34:54.230 --> 01:34:55.750
but can the sum

01:34:55.750 --> 01:34:58.250
of all values of commodities

01:34:58.250 --> 01:35:00.863
be different from the sum of all prices?

01:35:03.160 --> 01:35:04.790
- Okay, thank you.

01:35:04.790 --> 01:35:06.410
You know, this is a beautiful opportunity

01:35:06.410 --> 01:35:08.750
where you can ask both
questions about the end

01:35:08.750 --> 01:35:11.930
of the world and incredibly
technical questions.

01:35:11.930 --> 01:35:16.463
And professor Heinrich
handles them both with grace.

01:35:16.463 --> 01:35:17.570
Do you want to start

01:35:17.570 --> 01:35:20.363
with these or do you want to
ask, do you want some more?

01:35:21.930 --> 01:35:24.900
- Maybe one more question.

01:35:24.900 --> 01:35:26.533
- Great, so.

01:35:30.240 --> 01:35:31.440
Okay, this is a little bit,

01:35:31.440 --> 01:35:34.140
little bit off topic,
but let's do it anyway.

01:35:34.140 --> 01:35:36.643
Could you Audrey open Jack Copely?

01:35:41.800 --> 01:35:42.993
Jack, are you there?

01:35:47.040 --> 01:35:48.140
Reminds me of all the hours.

01:35:48.140 --> 01:35:51.110
I spent listening to
talk radio in my life.

01:35:51.110 --> 01:35:54.033
They have someone who makes
sure the person is there.

01:35:55.650 --> 01:35:59.713
I can read Jack's question if
he's not on the list anymore.

01:36:04.490 --> 01:36:06.170
What do you think Audrey?

01:36:06.170 --> 01:36:09.130
- Someone in the chat said
that his mic does not work.

01:36:09.130 --> 01:36:11.270
- Okay so then I'll read the question.

01:36:11.270 --> 01:36:12.103
Sorry.

01:36:13.320 --> 01:36:15.600
He says, "Wonderful
talk, before capitalism

01:36:15.600 --> 01:36:18.280
there existed in certain places and times

01:36:18.280 --> 01:36:20.370
money, wage labor, market exchange,

01:36:20.370 --> 01:36:22.450
private property, et cetera.

01:36:22.450 --> 01:36:24.310
Can we say that abstract labor

01:36:24.310 --> 01:36:27.200
and the law of value
existed in incipient form

01:36:27.200 --> 01:36:30.257
on the margins of such
pre capitalist societies?"

01:36:31.230 --> 01:36:32.700
Oh, he says his microphone doesn't work.

01:36:32.700 --> 01:36:33.583
I'm so sorry.

01:36:34.660 --> 01:36:36.140
I see it now.

01:36:36.140 --> 01:36:40.680
Okay, Michael I'll let
you go for those four.

01:36:40.680 --> 01:36:45.250
- Yeah, I will start
with the last question.

01:36:45.250 --> 01:36:49.220
It is a very important question.

01:36:49.220 --> 01:36:52.440
We are very used to say money,

01:36:52.440 --> 01:36:57.440
commodities, even capital or
private property exists long,

01:36:58.020 --> 01:37:00.043
long ago before capitalism.

01:37:01.030 --> 01:37:04.110
This is not completely true.

01:37:04.110 --> 01:37:09.110
We have to consider that all
this, property, money, capital

01:37:11.641 --> 01:37:15.883
in pre capitalist societies is different.

01:37:16.940 --> 01:37:21.553
You have the capitalist
property in nowadays.

01:37:23.790 --> 01:37:26.500
What, what is common

01:37:26.500 --> 01:37:31.500
to us is defined by the
exclusion of others.

01:37:31.860 --> 01:37:35.490
When you want to define what is property,

01:37:35.490 --> 01:37:40.490
private property, I can exclude
anybody else from the use.

01:37:41.010 --> 01:37:43.640
This is my ground.

01:37:43.640 --> 01:37:46.090
I don't allow you to use it.

01:37:46.090 --> 01:37:47.960
Then it is my property.

01:37:47.960 --> 01:37:50.827
This is a rather new constellation

01:37:54.200 --> 01:37:57.380
in all pre capitalist societies

01:37:57.380 --> 01:38:00.850
you had something you
can call private property

01:38:00.850 --> 01:38:05.300
but with social obligations, the exclusion

01:38:05.300 --> 01:38:10.300
of the others was not the
common feature of property.

01:38:11.920 --> 01:38:13.733
Also with money.

01:38:14.860 --> 01:38:19.860
When you see some coins,
you think, oh, that's money.

01:38:20.050 --> 01:38:23.853
But also this is not true.

01:38:24.690 --> 01:38:27.120
You have, on the one hand

01:38:27.120 --> 01:38:32.120
the use of this coin followed
in parts, different rules

01:38:34.110 --> 01:38:35.890
according to the situation,

01:38:35.890 --> 01:38:40.180
it is money, nowadays is abstract wealth.

01:38:40.180 --> 01:38:42.130
But the so-called money

01:38:42.130 --> 01:38:46.400
in pre capitalist times
was something bound

01:38:47.320 --> 01:38:50.543
in certain social relations.

01:38:53.340 --> 01:38:57.917
Even what we nowadays call
functions of money were

01:38:59.780 --> 01:39:04.780
in some societies distributed
to different bearers

01:39:06.030 --> 01:39:11.030
a mean of circulation, means
of circulation was different

01:39:12.520 --> 01:39:17.333
from measure of value or
from a storage of value.

01:39:18.200 --> 01:39:23.200
So you cannot just say, "Oh,
this exists all the time".

01:39:24.970 --> 01:39:26.700
And in so far

01:39:26.700 --> 01:39:31.700
I would also say there was not
a double character of labor

01:39:33.530 --> 01:39:37.170
like the analysis with
capitalist commodity.

01:39:37.170 --> 01:39:41.683
And of course not in the
same way, abstract labor.

01:39:42.570 --> 01:39:44.940
And this point is even discussed

01:39:44.940 --> 01:39:48.870
very briefly in "Capital"
in the first chapter

01:39:48.870 --> 01:39:52.613
when Marx made this excursus on Aristotle.

01:39:54.680 --> 01:39:59.680
Why Aristotle couldn't
come to value theory.

01:40:02.460 --> 01:40:07.460
He was restricted by the
structures of his society.

01:40:08.560 --> 01:40:09.940
And one could add this,

01:40:09.940 --> 01:40:14.190
he was not that something
existed and he couldn't see, no,

01:40:14.190 --> 01:40:19.190
it didn't exist in the
way it exists nowadays.

01:40:19.370 --> 01:40:23.430
And this is also important when we, now,

01:40:23.430 --> 01:40:25.670
especially with the Marxist approach

01:40:27.050 --> 01:40:29.600
analyze pre capitalist societies

01:40:29.600 --> 01:40:33.190
we must be extremely cautious

01:40:33.190 --> 01:40:37.650
not just to project our categories

01:40:37.650 --> 01:40:40.520
on the pre capitalist society.

01:40:40.520 --> 01:40:45.053
Then our analysis will go very wrong.

01:40:46.390 --> 01:40:48.843
So now the second.

01:40:50.680 --> 01:40:51.670
Ah, no, no, no, no,

01:40:51.670 --> 01:40:56.670
the third I can do it
really the opposite order.

01:40:56.800 --> 01:41:01.070
The third question value
differs from price.

01:41:01.070 --> 01:41:02.860
And you asked can also the sum

01:41:02.860 --> 01:41:06.263
of value differ from the sum of prices.

01:41:07.710 --> 01:41:12.520
I don't know how you mean this question.

01:41:12.520 --> 01:41:16.460
There is the so-called
transformation problem.

01:41:16.460 --> 01:41:20.730
It is between value
theory, as it is presented

01:41:20.730 --> 01:41:24.210
in volume one in volume
three, we have a theory

01:41:24.210 --> 01:41:29.210
of production prices
and Marx tries to show

01:41:31.230 --> 01:41:36.230
that production prices
still ruled by values.

01:41:36.470 --> 01:41:39.150
He even offers a way

01:41:39.150 --> 01:41:44.150
of accounting, how we can
account starting with values,

01:41:44.350 --> 01:41:49.140
the production prices and
the average rate of profit

01:41:50.570 --> 01:41:54.100
but this accounting is
wrong, what he presents

01:41:54.100 --> 01:41:57.310
and the ironic point is that he knew this.

01:41:57.310 --> 01:41:58.330
He mentioned this

01:41:58.330 --> 01:42:01.210
that this is wrong, but he
said it doesn't play a role

01:42:01.210 --> 01:42:05.440
because he didn't want to go in details.

01:42:05.440 --> 01:42:06.830
Later on, it came out,

01:42:06.830 --> 01:42:11.830
it played a much bigger role
than Marx expected this.

01:42:12.820 --> 01:42:13.653
I don't know

01:42:13.653 --> 01:42:17.970
if you have this in mind,
when you have this in mind

01:42:17.970 --> 01:42:20.155
I must say, now is the time to show,

01:42:20.155 --> 01:42:25.095
to come to the transformation problem

01:42:25.095 --> 01:42:28.750
then I first would have to
explain what this means.

01:42:28.750 --> 01:42:33.750
I wrote about this in "Signs
of Value", for example.

01:42:36.951 --> 01:42:41.951
So I must put the answer to the book.

01:42:42.760 --> 01:42:45.637
When you just stay on the level

01:42:45.637 --> 01:42:49.390
of volume one where I said,
yes, money expresses value

01:42:49.390 --> 01:42:52.350
and it can express more or less value.

01:42:52.350 --> 01:42:57.350
Then it is for sure that
there is no necessary equation

01:42:58.010 --> 01:42:58.880
that the sum

01:42:58.880 --> 01:43:03.880
of all prices is equal
to the sum of all values.

01:43:05.640 --> 01:43:09.283
It can be more, it can be less.

01:43:11.690 --> 01:43:16.690
So second question about book four.

01:43:17.030 --> 01:43:21.573
Here, first, we have to
distinguish, you identify it.

01:43:24.820 --> 01:43:28.363
Book four, with theories of surplus value.

01:43:29.320 --> 01:43:31.200
This is wrong.

01:43:31.200 --> 01:43:33.733
This is definitely wrong.

01:43:35.440 --> 01:43:37.250
Book for of "Capital".

01:43:37.250 --> 01:43:42.050
The history of theories was never written.

01:43:42.050 --> 01:43:44.700
There is not even a draft,

01:43:44.700 --> 01:43:47.350
theories of surplus value

01:43:47.350 --> 01:43:52.350
as the name already
indicates is just the history

01:43:53.910 --> 01:43:57.720
of one category of surplus value.

01:43:57.720 --> 01:44:02.650
But book four should be the
history of economic theory

01:44:02.650 --> 01:44:06.363
what is a different thing.

01:44:07.790 --> 01:44:10.900
Furthermore, the history of theory

01:44:11.910 --> 01:44:16.780
Marx wanted to write when
he had all his categories.

01:44:16.780 --> 01:44:21.690
So presupposing the
three theoretical books

01:44:21.690 --> 01:44:22.830
and the categories.

01:44:22.830 --> 01:44:27.491
He wanted to write the
history of the theory.

01:44:27.491 --> 01:44:29.580
When Marx wrote theories

01:44:29.580 --> 01:44:34.390
of surplus value, he didn't
have all the categories.

01:44:34.390 --> 01:44:36.670
It emerged in the big manuscript

01:44:36.670 --> 01:44:40.630
written between 1861 to 63

01:44:40.630 --> 01:44:44.253
where he was in the middle
of his research process.

01:44:46.028 --> 01:44:51.028
And this research process continued also

01:44:51.230 --> 01:44:55.570
after finishing theories of surplus value.

01:44:55.570 --> 01:45:00.210
The research process continued

01:45:00.210 --> 01:45:04.613
on the one hand regarding
the theoretical categories

01:45:08.880 --> 01:45:12.820
but it also continued on the field

01:45:12.820 --> 01:45:17.613
of the history of economic theories.

01:45:19.240 --> 01:45:20.940
In the year,

01:45:20.940 --> 01:45:25.940
at the end of the year, 1863
Marx produced a new notebook.

01:45:28.690 --> 01:45:32.813
The so-called "Bi Haftar"
accompanying notebooks.

01:45:35.720 --> 01:45:40.720
A to G, they were numbered
by letters from A to G,

01:45:42.720 --> 01:45:47.480
in this "Bi Haftar", they
are not published until now.

01:45:47.480 --> 01:45:50.310
They will come in in mega of course,

01:45:50.310 --> 01:45:55.310
but in this "Bi Haftar",
he widened his view

01:45:59.536 --> 01:46:03.290
on economic theory enormously.

01:46:03.290 --> 01:46:08.290
So, "Theories of Surplus Value",
luck in several dimensions

01:46:11.050 --> 01:46:13.713
something what would make them even

01:46:13.713 --> 01:46:18.713
to a draft for book four,
it is only one category.

01:46:19.290 --> 01:46:23.963
The system of categories
of the theory is not ready.

01:46:25.110 --> 01:46:30.090
And the research in the history

01:46:30.090 --> 01:46:35.090
of theory went on after
"Theories of Surplus Value".

01:46:38.870 --> 01:46:40.996
So "Theories of Surplus Value" is not

01:46:40.996 --> 01:46:45.996
the number four, that the book is missing,

01:46:46.400 --> 01:46:51.400
that this fourth book is
missing is really, really a blow

01:46:53.650 --> 01:46:57.990
because in "Capital" Marx always relates

01:46:59.930 --> 01:47:04.930
in footnotes, in small
remarks to Smith's and Ricardo

01:47:04.930 --> 01:47:09.930
but we have nowhere, a big
presentation about Smiths

01:47:13.780 --> 01:47:18.780
and Ricardo and such an
representation would be necessary

01:47:19.220 --> 01:47:22.170
especially when you have in mind

01:47:22.170 --> 01:47:23.610
what I said at the beginning

01:47:23.610 --> 01:47:28.610
that after "Capital" appeared,
the marginalists came

01:47:29.140 --> 01:47:33.943
and they rewrote the history
with these new distinctions.

01:47:35.290 --> 01:47:40.290
If Marx would have clearly
defined his relation

01:47:40.630 --> 01:47:43.930
to the classics more than in
the footnotes of "Capital"

01:47:43.930 --> 01:47:48.587
it would be much more easy
to say clearly the framework

01:47:50.060 --> 01:47:52.420
what the marginalist offer for the history

01:47:52.420 --> 01:47:57.070
of theory is absolutely
not fitting for Marx.

01:47:57.070 --> 01:48:00.170
So that we miss book four

01:48:00.170 --> 01:48:03.540
has not only a scientific relevance

01:48:03.540 --> 01:48:07.120
it also has a political relevance dealing

01:48:07.120 --> 01:48:09.913
with competing theories.

01:48:11.240 --> 01:48:13.803
So, and now I,

01:48:15.380 --> 01:48:20.380
yeah, Paul Reitter is left.

01:48:23.100 --> 01:48:28.100
Misreadings popular versions,
in the secondary edition

01:48:30.040 --> 01:48:33.607
you have mainly as value form analysis

01:48:39.060 --> 01:48:41.910
a reworking of the appendix

01:48:41.910 --> 01:48:46.350
of the first volume, which
was already popularized

01:48:46.350 --> 01:48:51.350
which also, I must say
has one big progress,

01:48:51.800 --> 01:48:54.100
the three peculiarities

01:48:54.100 --> 01:48:56.120
of the equivalent form appear

01:48:56.120 --> 01:48:58.280
for the first time in the appendix.

01:48:58.280 --> 01:49:01.510
This is really a progress compared with

01:49:02.560 --> 01:49:05.643
the version of the first
chapter, first edition,

01:49:06.630 --> 01:49:11.630
but that the force value form
of the first edition is missed

01:49:15.250 --> 01:49:18.403
or is replaced in the second edition.

01:49:19.900 --> 01:49:23.070
This is a big problem I think.

01:49:23.070 --> 01:49:26.510
This is a lack what we have

01:49:26.510 --> 01:49:31.210
and there are also some historising

01:49:34.478 --> 01:49:36.850
insertions that in value form analysis

01:49:37.690 --> 01:49:42.290
Marx starts with historical examples.

01:49:42.290 --> 01:49:46.440
They only shall illustrate value form

01:49:46.440 --> 01:49:49.900
or the different value forms, but this

01:49:49.900 --> 01:49:54.900
of course supports this
historical reading of value form.

01:49:55.750 --> 01:49:59.493
In the first edition,
you didn't have this,

01:50:02.210 --> 01:50:05.667
then you have popularized remarks

01:50:08.880 --> 01:50:12.000
also in other parts of the book

01:50:12.000 --> 01:50:15.890
but they came not so much
from the second edition.

01:50:15.890 --> 01:50:19.130
They come from the fourth edition,

01:50:19.130 --> 01:50:21.243
the fourth edition of volume one.

01:50:23.150 --> 01:50:26.560
Not prepared by Marx, but by Engels.

01:50:26.560 --> 01:50:29.483
And what did Engels do in this edition?

01:50:31.150 --> 01:50:35.730
He took the second edition,
the second German edition

01:50:35.730 --> 01:50:40.730
and looked what are the
differences in the French edition

01:50:43.870 --> 01:50:46.140
and also he had a small list

01:50:46.140 --> 01:50:48.990
of Marx of changes he wants to do

01:50:48.990 --> 01:50:53.990
for a third German edition or
for an English translation.

01:50:55.180 --> 01:50:58.763
And Engels try to combine this.

01:50:59.680 --> 01:51:04.050
Sometimes he did this very successfully.

01:51:04.050 --> 01:51:08.630
Sometimes he did this not so successfully.

01:51:08.630 --> 01:51:13.630
And the strictness in which Marx dealt

01:51:13.770 --> 01:51:18.040
with the category was
a little bit blurred.

01:51:20.660 --> 01:51:24.690
In one case, there is even
a translation mistake.

01:51:26.250 --> 01:51:30.000
Engels had to translate
from the French edition

01:51:30.000 --> 01:51:34.460
to the German when he
wanted to insert something

01:51:34.460 --> 01:51:37.900
because there was not a German original

01:51:37.900 --> 01:51:39.830
of the French edition.

01:51:39.830 --> 01:51:44.830
And there is one small mistake.

01:51:47.290 --> 01:51:51.980
Okay, the sense delivers nonsense

01:51:51.980 --> 01:51:54.603
in the section on accumulation.

01:51:55.590 --> 01:51:59.740
And it seems that nobody realized

01:51:59.740 --> 01:52:02.500
that literally speaking
the the sense is nonsense

01:52:02.500 --> 01:52:07.500
but these were very small points.

01:52:08.020 --> 01:52:13.020
The more decisive point is
the overwhelming presence

01:52:14.800 --> 01:52:17.690
of book one

01:52:17.690 --> 01:52:22.080
the overwhelming presence
of the production process

01:52:22.080 --> 01:52:26.573
which structured the
perception of "Capital".

01:52:29.530 --> 01:52:34.380
So I wouldn't say there
are so much misreadings

01:52:34.380 --> 01:52:37.300
or so much phrases

01:52:37.300 --> 01:52:42.300
in the second edition which
giving cause to misreadings.

01:52:43.420 --> 01:52:47.570
I would more stress this special

01:52:47.570 --> 01:52:51.920
reception putting production
process in the center.

01:52:51.920 --> 01:52:54.313
This caused a big misreading.

01:52:58.050 --> 01:52:59.613
- Okay, thank you.

01:53:00.970 --> 01:53:04.240
It is about our closing time now.

01:53:04.240 --> 01:53:07.290
There's been, there are some
more questions in the chat

01:53:07.290 --> 01:53:11.220
and there's been some requests
that one question get aired.

01:53:11.220 --> 01:53:12.780
And so I'm gonna air that question

01:53:12.780 --> 01:53:15.723
as the last question with
apologies to everyone else.

01:53:18.320 --> 01:53:20.540
That is a question from Lucas Guesser

01:53:20.540 --> 01:53:22.940
and I'm just gonna read
it for the sake of time.

01:53:24.437 --> 01:53:26.210
"There's been a lot of debate recently

01:53:26.210 --> 01:53:27.670
in Brazilian economics

01:53:27.670 --> 01:53:30.653
about the so-called
modern monetary theory.

01:53:31.630 --> 01:53:32.960
Do you think it is compatible

01:53:32.960 --> 01:53:35.360
with Marx's analysis of capitalism?"

01:53:35.360 --> 01:53:38.440
Make this the last question
with great apologies

01:53:38.440 --> 01:53:42.990
to all the people with also
great questions in the chat.

01:53:42.990 --> 01:53:43.910
Michael?

01:53:43.910 --> 01:53:48.630
- Yeah, this is a good question

01:53:50.480 --> 01:53:55.480
but I would say we have to put this

01:53:57.390 --> 01:54:01.610
in a little bit in a broader range.

01:54:01.610 --> 01:54:04.530
Modern monetary theory,

01:54:04.530 --> 01:54:07.950
now the time is too short to say something

01:54:07.950 --> 01:54:11.750
what it is for the people
who don't know the term

01:54:11.750 --> 01:54:15.753
it comes from some rather radical pupils

01:54:17.550 --> 01:54:21.550
of Keynes from Keynesian school.

01:54:21.550 --> 01:54:23.870
And there is the broader question

01:54:23.870 --> 01:54:28.630
Keynesian theory and Marx on
critical political economy.

01:54:28.630 --> 01:54:31.580
Do they fit together or not?

01:54:31.580 --> 01:54:36.580
And here, I think we have
this question is prior

01:54:36.750 --> 01:54:41.750
and here we have to deal
with Keynes in a similar way.

01:54:42.130 --> 01:54:44.960
We have to deal with Marx.

01:54:44.960 --> 01:54:46.730
With Marx I stress

01:54:46.730 --> 01:54:48.660
we cannot take everything for

01:54:48.660 --> 01:54:52.330
granted what the different Marxist

01:54:52.330 --> 01:54:55.260
traditions tell us about Marx.

01:54:55.260 --> 01:55:00.260
We have to see which
categories really fit to Marx.

01:55:00.760 --> 01:55:05.760
The same is true for Keynesian economics.

01:55:06.190 --> 01:55:09.000
Keynes was very often reduced

01:55:09.000 --> 01:55:11.440
to a deficit spending policy.

01:55:11.440 --> 01:55:12.770
And in some respect

01:55:13.850 --> 01:55:17.420
this modern monetary
theory is a radicalization

01:55:17.420 --> 01:55:19.510
of this approach

01:55:20.430 --> 01:55:25.430
that we don't have to fear
deficits that deficits are good

01:55:27.861 --> 01:55:30.023
for the development of the economy.

01:55:31.800 --> 01:55:36.800
Keynes has to offer a much
more elaborated analysis

01:55:39.690 --> 01:55:41.980
but like Marx also

01:55:41.980 --> 01:55:46.980
in manuscripts not published
during his lifetime.

01:55:47.280 --> 01:55:51.710
During his lifetime was in the year 1931,

01:55:51.710 --> 01:55:56.710
the treaties on money
where he was half with one

01:55:56.783 --> 01:55:59.700
leg fixed in neoclassical theory

01:55:59.700 --> 01:56:02.310
and only with one leg beyond.

01:56:02.310 --> 01:56:04.650
And then in 1936,

01:56:04.650 --> 01:56:09.650
the general theory where he
tried to formulate a difference,

01:56:11.100 --> 01:56:16.100
a basic difference to
neo-classical mainstream

01:56:17.300 --> 01:56:20.990
but general theory, the published version

01:56:20.990 --> 01:56:25.990
was already a popularized,
moderated version

01:56:28.730 --> 01:56:33.730
of manuscripts which
emerged between 31 and 36.

01:56:33.780 --> 01:56:36.060
There he developed a theory,

01:56:36.060 --> 01:56:40.140
a monetary theory of production.

01:56:40.140 --> 01:56:45.140
And this monetary theory of
production, I think has points

01:56:45.830 --> 01:56:50.830
you can connect it very easily with Marx.

01:56:51.470 --> 01:56:55.120
So there is the possibility
to connect something

01:56:55.120 --> 01:57:00.027
but not on a superficial sphere.

01:57:01.290 --> 01:57:04.320
And with modern monetary theory

01:57:05.200 --> 01:57:07.730
I would say they have some good

01:57:07.730 --> 01:57:11.830
points criticizing the mainstream

01:57:11.830 --> 01:57:14.790
but what they have to offer

01:57:14.790 --> 01:57:19.790
as a theory until now
is not really a theory.

01:57:21.110 --> 01:57:22.950
It is some ideas about money

01:57:22.950 --> 01:57:27.950
and the state and the state
debt which is rather premature.

01:57:27.980 --> 01:57:31.300
So it is good that this approach exists.

01:57:31.300 --> 01:57:35.830
It inspired a lot the discussion

01:57:35.830 --> 01:57:38.770
but I think it is not something you

01:57:38.770 --> 01:57:43.770
can just easily integrate
in the Marxian framework.

01:57:43.950 --> 01:57:46.210
But to repeat this,

01:57:46.210 --> 01:57:50.470
the radical side of Keynes' writings,

01:57:50.470 --> 01:57:51.759
which were not published

01:57:51.759 --> 01:57:55.070
in his lifetime, the manuscript were found

01:57:55.070 --> 01:57:56.928
after his widow died

01:57:56.928 --> 01:58:00.230
in the 70s and only then
they were published.

01:58:00.230 --> 01:58:04.760
These manuscripts are very interesting for

01:58:05.620 --> 01:58:09.320
when you occupy this
critic of political economy

01:58:09.320 --> 01:58:11.500
in my "Science of value",

01:58:11.500 --> 01:58:14.680
which finally will appear next year

01:58:14.680 --> 01:58:19.300
I make some remarks to these manuscripts.

01:58:19.300 --> 01:58:23.223
For the moment I will stop at this point.

01:58:24.660 --> 01:58:25.493
- Thank you very much.

01:58:25.493 --> 01:58:28.580
Again, this is where the
applause track would be.

01:58:28.580 --> 01:58:29.700
We will be waiting

01:58:29.700 --> 01:58:32.270
for the English version
of "The Science of Value".

01:58:32.270 --> 01:58:36.600
We appreciate very much your fortitude

01:58:36.600 --> 01:58:39.190
and your willingness
to answer all questions

01:58:39.190 --> 01:58:43.390
your movement from the
technical to the revolutionary

01:58:43.390 --> 01:58:46.810
to the historical, and really
is, it's a singular thing.

01:58:46.810 --> 01:58:48.260
So I appreciate it very much.

01:58:48.260 --> 01:58:49.310
Thank you for coming.

